window.PT_INDEX = [
{p:"start.html",a:"",t:"Getting started",h:"",k:"first time getting started begin overview how it works quick start steps Use it for the first time The five steps, start to finish.",x:"Five steps: dump, edit, save, load, regenerate."},
{p:"start.html",a:"what",t:"Getting started",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"In Mastercam you change one operation at a time, one dialog at a time. This tool puts every op of your part into one Excel workbook, one row per op. You change feeds, speeds, depths, coolant and comments there – many at once if you like. Excel shows you the new cycle time and insert use as you type. Then the tool puts your changes back into the part. It shows you every change first, and nothing goes in until you say so."},
{p:"start.html",a:"when",t:"Getting started",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"You want the part to run faster, or you were given a cycle time to hit. Inserts are wearing out too soon, or cost too much. You need the same change on many ops (coolant, a feed, a comment). You want to look over a whole program before it goes to the floor."},
{p:"start.html",a:"steps",t:"Getting started",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Save the part in Mastercam. The tool works on the saved part file. Dump. Click Lathe params - dump to Excel. Pick the ops, the folder and the name, then click Dump. The workbook opens in Excel. See Dump to Excel. Edit. Change cells on the Lathe params sheet. Every cell you change turns yellow. The cycle time and flips on that row move as you type. See The main sheet. Save the workbook in Excel (Ctrl+S). Keep it as it is – do not “Save As” CSV. Load. Back in Mastercam, click Lathe params - load and pick the workbook. A window lists every change. Untick anything you want to leave out, then click Apply. See Load the changes. Regenerate the changed ops in Mastercam, check them (backplot, verify), and save the part. If it is not right, run Lathe params - undo last load. An empty cell means “leave it alone” – never zero. If you clear a cell by mistake, the load does not change that setting."},
{p:"start.html",a:"example",t:"Getting started",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"The Oil Spool Head sample part has 18 ops and a cycle time of 12:46:38. The rough bore (op 7) runs at 0.010 inch per rev. You think the insert can take more. Dump all ops. The workbook opens on the Summary page. On Lathe params, find op 7 and type 0.012 in its feed cell. Do the same for op 8, the finish bore. Look at the Summary page: the cycle time is now 12:26:50. Save, load, Apply, regenerate ops 7 and 8. What changed Op What Before After 7 Rough Bore ID feed (per rev) 0.010 0.012 7 Rough Bore ID time 1:44:00 1:26:45 8 Finish Bore ID feed (per rev) 0.010 0.012 8 Finish Bore ID time 0:15:24 0:12:50 Whole part cycle time 12:46:38 12:26:50 (−0:19:49) (1) The feed you typed turns yellow. (2) Mastercam's own time stays as it was. (3) The estimate moves – blue means “this moved because of your edit”."},
{p:"start.html",a:"related",t:"Getting started",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Install the add-in What is in the workbook How the cycle time estimate works Word list"},
{p:"install.html",a:"",t:"Install the add-in",h:"",k:"install setup add-in addin download zip Add-Ins folder customize ribbon quick access toolbar enable macros enable content",x:"A file copy. No installer, no admin rights."},
{p:"install.html",a:"what",t:"Install the add-in",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"The tool is a Mastercam 2026 add-in. Installing it puts four commands into Mastercam: Lathe params - dump to Excel, Lathe params - load, Lathe params - undo last load and Parameter Table - user manual. This manual comes with it."},
{p:"install.html",a:"when",t:"Install the add-in",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"Once per computer, and again when a new version comes out. Ask your CAD/CAM lead or IT if you are not allowed to add programs – some shops want to look at add-ins first."},
{p:"install.html",a:"steps",t:"Install the add-in",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Download the release .zip file and unzip it (right-click it, Extract All). Inside are ParamTable.ft and a folder called ParamTable. Close Mastercam. It only looks for add-ins when it starts. Open the folder Documents\\My Mastercam 2026\\Mastercam\\Add-Ins. Copy ParamTable.ft and the ParamTable folder into it. You need both. Start Mastercam. A Parameter Table tab is on the ribbon with the four commands: dump to Excel, load, undo the last load, and this user manual. Nothing to set up. (1) The Parameter Table tab. (2) Dump to Excel. (3) Load. (4) The user manual. Want them somewhere else? The commands are also in Mastercam's Customize list, in the category Parameter Table Tool - put them on a tab of your own or on the Quick Access Toolbar. If you put them on a tab of your own, Mastercam shows yours and does not add the Parameter Table tab as well. Macros in Excel. If you tick Include macros when you dump, Excel may show a yellow bar: Macros have been disabled. Click Enable Content on that bar to get the Parameter Table tab. The workbook works without macros too – you just do not get the extra tools."},
{p:"install.html",a:"example",t:"Install the add-in",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"The Add-Ins folder before and after Before After (other add-ins, if any) (other add-ins, if any) ParamTable.ft ParamTable\\ParamTable.dll ParamTable\\help\\ (this manual) ParamTable\\SHA256SUMS.txt, BUILD-INFO.txt, LICENSE.txt After Mastercam starts, the ribbon has a Parameter Table tab with four buttons."},
{p:"install.html",a:"messages",t:"Install the add-in",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"There is no Parameter Table tab. Mastercam was open when you copied, or ParamTable.ft is not in the Add-Ins folder itself (it must not be inside the ParamTable folder). Fix it and restart Mastercam. If you already put the commands on a tab of your own with Customize, that tab is the one you get. Still nothing? The file ParamTable-ribbon.log in Add-Ins\\ParamTable says what happened at start-up - send it to whoever looks after the add-in. The user manual is not installed. The help folder is missing from Add-Ins\\ParamTable. Copy the whole ParamTable folder again from the zip."},
{p:"install.html",a:"related",t:"Install the add-in",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Getting started Dump to Excel Troubleshooting"},
{p:"dump.html",a:"",t:"Dump to Excel",h:"",k:"dump export excel workbook xlsx xlsm save to file name pick operations saved ticks include macros tool pictures simulate stock",x:"Mastercam: Lathe params - dump to Excel"},
{p:"dump.html",a:"what",t:"Dump to Excel",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"The dump writes your part's ops into one Excel workbook. A window asks which ops, which folder, and what to call the file. It never writes over a file that is already there."},
{p:"dump.html",a:"when",t:"Dump to Excel",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"Every time you start working on a part in Excel. Again after a load and a regenerate, so the sheet shows the new times. To look over a program without changing anything (dump, read, close)."},
{p:"dump.html",a:"steps",t:"Dump to Excel",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Save the part. Then click Lathe params - dump to Excel. Pick the ops. Every op is listed by toolpath group, with a tick box. Use the buttons: All, None, Selected in Operation Manager, or pick a kind (ROUGH, FINISH ...) and click Tick kind / Untick kind. Or find them. Type part of a comment, a tool, a type or an op number in Find. Matching ops light up. Click Tick matches. Keep a pick for next time (if you like): type a name under Saved ticks and click Save ticks. Next time, pick the name to tick the same ops. Where: under Save to, click Beside the part or Browse.... Name: leave File name as it is, or change it. The line under it shows the name you will get. Options: tick Include macros (.xlsm) to get the Parameter Table tab in Excel (most of this manual needs it). Leave the other boxes ticked. Click Dump (it says how many ops, e.g. Dump 18 operations). The workbook opens in Excel. (1) The ops. (2) Find. (3) Save to. (4) File name. (5) Include macros. (6) Dump. The file name The name can hold these words in braces; the tool fills them in: Type You get {part} the part's file name, e.g. Oil Spool Head_2026 {date} {time} today's date and the time, e.g. 20261009 155309 {scope} all, or selected when you dump only some ops {ops} how many ops, e.g. 18 If a file by that name is already there, the new one gets (2) on the end. Nothing is ever written over."},
{p:"dump.html",a:"example",t:"Dump to Excel",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"You only want to work on the boring ops of the Oil Spool Head. In Find, type bore. Two ops light up: 7 Rough Bore ID and 8 Finish Bore ID. Click None, then Tick matches. Type Bore ops under Saved ticks and click Save ticks. Click Dump 2 operations. Before After Ops ticked 18 (all) 2 (ops 7 and 8) Button Dump 18 operations Dump 2 operations Saved ticks (none) Bore ops Dump the whole part when you can. The Summary page and the Tools page add up only the ops in the workbook, so a part-dump gives the true cycle time and flips."},
{p:"dump.html",a:"messages",t:"Dump to Excel",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"Save the part first ... The part has never been saved. Save it, then dump again. This part has no operations of a kind this tool reads ... The tool reads lathe ops (rough, finish, face, groove, drill, PrimeTurning, dynamic rough, manual entry ...) and some mill ops (contour, drill, dynamic mill). Other kinds are listed as Not read yet (left out). Could not write ... Check the folder can be written to ... The folder is read-only, full, or on a network drive that went away. Pick another folder. that folder does not exist (under the file name) Fix the Save to folder, or click Beside the part. Dumped 18 operation(s) ... Done. It says how many of each kind, and where the file went."},
{p:"dump.html",a:"related",t:"Dump to Excel",h:"Related",k:"",x:"What is in the workbook Load the changes The part's .ptconfig file (what you typed, kept for the next dump) Troubleshooting"},
{p:"batch.html",a:"",t:"Dump a folder of parts",h:"",k:"batch folder many parts all parts shop workbook compare standards insert median range sfm feed depth edge time cycle time insert cost subfolders Shop_compare log",x:"Mastercam: Parameter Table - dump a folder of parts"},
{p:"batch.html",a:"what",t:"Dump a folder of parts",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"This dumps every part in a folder, one after another. Each part gets its own workbook, the same as Dump to Excel makes. Then one more workbook puts all the parts side by side: the shop workbook. Use it to see how your shop runs each insert across many parts: the speeds, the feeds, the depths of cut and how long an edge lasts. That is where shop standards start - not one part at a time, but the whole shop."},
{p:"batch.html",a:"when",t:"Dump a folder of parts",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"To find the parts that run an insert slower (or harder) than the rest of the shop. To start, or check, your shop's usual speed, feed and edge time for each insert. To compare the cycle times and insert costs of a family of parts. Not to edit a part. For that, use Dump to Excel on the one part."},
{p:"batch.html",a:"before",t:"Dump a folder of parts",h:"Before you start",k:"",x:"Save your part. Mastercam has only one part open at a time. The tool closes your part, opens each part of the folder in turn, and opens your part again at the end. If your part has changes that are not saved, the tool stops and tells you. Nothing in the parts changes. Each part is opened, dumped and closed. It is not regenerated and never saved. An op that needs regenerating is dumped with its old toolpath. The Parts sheet counts them (ops_needing_regen). Every op of every part is dumped. Op kinds the tool does not read yet are left out and named."},
{p:"batch.html",a:"steps",t:"Dump a folder of parts",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Save your part. Then click Parameter Table - dump a folder of parts. Read the note under the title (1). It names your part: it is closed now and opened again at the end. Parts: type the folder, or click Browse... (2). Tick Include subfolders to take the folders inside it too. The green line (3) says how many parts it found. Save the workbooks: leave Beside each part, as a normal dump (4). Or pick All in one folder and browse to it. Options: leave them as they are. Simulate the stock (5) is off: it is slow, and with it off the tool uses Mastercam's own stock boundaries for the material removed. Click Dump (6) - it says how many parts, e.g. Dump 6 parts. A small window follows along: Part 3 of 6. To stop early, click Stop after this part. The parts done so far are kept. At the end a message says how many parts were dumped, and which were not and why. Click Yes to open the shop workbook in Excel. (1) The note: your part is closed and opened again. (2) The folder. (3) Parts found. (4) Where the workbooks go. (5) Simulate the stock, off. (6) Dump."},
{p:"batch.html",a:"shop",t:"Dump a folder of parts",h:"What is in the shop workbook",k:"",x:"It is called Shop_compare_ and the date, e.g. Shop_compare_20261010.xlsx. It goes in the folder of parts (or in the one folder you picked). It never writes over a file: a second one on the same day gets (2) on the end. Every sheet has filter arrows on its headings. Sheet One row per What you read there Parts part Dumped or not, and why. Ops, cycle time, cutting time, flips per part, and insert cost per part when the costs are typed on its Tools page. Inserts insert, kind of op and material The parts and tools that use it. The lowest, median and highest SFM, feed per rev, depth of cut and edge time. Ops op of every part Speed and feed as dumped, the same in inch units, depth, times, flips, edge time, MRR and air. About column What each column means, and its units. One set of units. So inch and metric parts can be compared, speeds are in SFM, feeds in inches per rev and depths in inches. A metric part's m/min, mm/rev and mm are changed over. The Ops sheet also keeps each op's own values. Edge time is how long one edge of a tool cuts before it is turned, in seconds: the tool's cutting time per part over its flips per part - the same way the Program check works it out. A log goes beside it, with the same name and .log: one line for each thing done to each part. Read it when a part was not dumped."},
{p:"batch.html",a:"example",t:"Dump a folder of parts",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"Your lathe cell has three parts in one folder: Bushing, Flange and Shaft. Each one finishes with a CNMG432 insert. You dump the folder. On the Inserts sheet, the CNMG432 FINISH row reads: insert op_kind n_ops parts sfm_min sfm_median sfm_max feed_median edge_time_median CNMG432 FINISH 3 3 450 600 650 0.006 150 450 is far below the median. Filter the Ops sheet to CNMG432 to see which part: part op sfm feed_ipr edge_seconds Bushing 4 450 0.006 210 Flange 7 600 0.005 150 Shaft 3 650 0.007 120 Bushing runs the insert at 450 SFM - 150 under the rest of the shop. Its edge lasts longest (3:30), so there is room to speed it up. Open Bushing in Mastercam and dump it. Use Speed & feed to try 600 SFM on op 4, and watch the cycle time. Shaft's edge lasts least (2:00) at the highest speed. Check it with the Program check. The median is the middle value. One odd part does not pull it far, so it is a good starting point for a shop standard."},
{p:"batch.html",a:"messages",t:"Dump a folder of parts",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"Save the part first ... Your part has changes that are not saved. Save it (or close it) and run the command again. 6 parts found (beside the folder) How many parts it will dump. no .mcam parts there: pick another folder, or tick Include subfolders. more than 2000 parts - is this the right folder?: you may have picked a whole drive. Dumped 5 of 6 parts. Not dumped: ... Each part that was not dumped is named, with why: Mastercam could not open it - the file is damaged, from a newer Mastercam, or open in another program. no operations of a kind this tool reads - for example a mill-only part. could not write ... - check the folder can be written to - the folder is read-only or full. the dump failed unexpectedly - ParamTable.log beside that part says how far it got. not reached - stopped (Cancel) - you clicked Stop after this part. Could not open ... again - open it with File > Open. Your part was not opened again at the end. Open it yourself. It was saved before the batch started, so nothing is lost. The shop workbook could not be written ... The folder is read-only or full. Each part's own workbook is still there. Pick another folder and run it again. A message from Mastercam while a part opens For example, a part saved in an older version. Answer it as you would when you open the part by hand. The batch then goes on."},
{p:"batch.html",a:"related",t:"Dump a folder of parts",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Dump to Excel (one part, with a choice of ops) Tools page (insert costs and the usual edge time) How insert flips are counted Program check The part's .ptconfig file (where insert costs are kept) Troubleshooting"},
{p:"load.html",a:"",t:"Load the changes",h:"",k:"load import apply write back preview tick untick refused impact cycle time flips regenerate",x:"Mastercam: Lathe params - load"},
{p:"load.html",a:"what",t:"Load the changes",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"The load reads your saved workbook and puts the changed values back into the ops. First it shows a window with every change, op by op: the old value crossed out in red, the new value in green. Each change has a tick box. Nothing is written until you click Apply."},
{p:"load.html",a:"when",t:"Load the changes",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"When you are happy with your edits in Excel and have saved the workbook."},
{p:"load.html",a:"steps",t:"Load the changes",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Save the workbook in Excel (Ctrl+S). You can leave it open. In Mastercam, open the same part and click Lathe params - load. Pick the workbook. The file window starts on the one you dumped last for this part. Click Open. Read the top line: the whole part's cycle time and insert flips, before and after. Read the list. Each op shows its own time and flips, then each change. Untick anything you want to leave out. Unticking an op unticks all of its changes. The top line follows. Look at the Refused part, if there is one. Those rows are not written at all (see below). Click Apply (it says how many, e.g. Apply 4 changes), or Cancel to write nothing. Regenerate the changed ops (they are marked dirty), check them, then save the part. (1) The whole part, before and after. (2) One op. (3) One change. (4) Its tick box. (5) Apply. Some changes tick together. A feed and its feed_mode (per rev / per min) go together; so do a speed and its CSS / RPM, and a value and the switch that turns it on. Half of a pair would make no sense."},
{p:"load.html",a:"example",t:"Load the changes",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"You changed the bore feeds (ops 7 and 8) and the facing speed (op 1). The preview shows: Operation Parameter Now Will be op 1 · FACE speed 200 250 op 7 · ROUGH feed 0.01 0.012 op 8 · FINISH feed 0.01 0.012 You decide to wait on the facing speed. Untick op 1. The top line drops back to the bore changes only. The button now reads Apply 2 changes. Click it; regenerate ops 7 and 8."},
{p:"load.html",a:"messages",t:"Load the changes",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"A refused row is left out whole – half a row is a state nobody chose. The reason is beside it: \"0.5\" is above the maximum ... / ... is below the minimum ... / ... is not a number A value is outside what Mastercam takes for that setting. Fix the cell, save, load again. \"...\" is not one of: ... A dropdown cell has something typed in that is not on its list (a typo in per rev, a coolant name). is 131 characters; the operation holds at most 119 A comment or text is too long. Shorten it – the tool will not cut it off for you. \"feed\" is read-only here but was changed ... You typed in a column the load never writes (a time, a count, an ID). Put the old value back. \"...\" does not apply to a FACE operation ... That cell is greyed out for that kind of op. Clear it. no ROUGH operation with that number is in the part / ... is a different kind of operation The workbook was dumped from another part, or the op was deleted or replaced since. Dump again. Save the part first ... No saved part is open in Mastercam. ... could not be opened (is it still open in Excel?) Save the workbook in Excel and try again. If it still fails, close it in Excel. Wrote 2 operation(s). They are marked for regeneration ... Done. Regenerate before you post, or the old values are still in the toolpath. Every old value goes into ParamTable.log beside the part first. That is how undo last load puts them back."},
{p:"load.html",a:"related",t:"Load the changes",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Undo a load List changes (check your edits before you load) How the cycle time estimate works Regenerate"},
{p:"undo-load.html",a:"",t:"Undo a load",h:"",k:"undo last load restore old values put back revert part ParamTable.log mistake Undo a load in Mastercam Put the old values back in the part.",x:"Mastercam: Lathe params - undo last load"},
{p:"undo-load.html",a:"what",t:"Undo a load",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"It takes the last load of this part back. The old values come from ParamTable.log, a file the load writes beside the part before it changes anything. You see the same kind of preview window as the load, with tick boxes, before anything is written. This is the undo for the part in Mastercam. For an undo inside Excel, see Undo."},
{p:"undo-load.html",a:"when",t:"Undo a load",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"After a load, when the result is not what you wanted: the backplot looks wrong, the program posts badly, or the operator says the new feed chatters. It is its own command so it is there later – long after the load window has closed."},
{p:"undo-load.html",a:"steps",t:"Undo a load",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Open the part in Mastercam (the same saved part file the load went into). Click Lathe params - undo last load. Read the window. Restore lists each value it will put back. Changed since the load lists values you changed by hand after the load – those are left alone. Untick anything you want to keep as it is now. Click Restore (e.g. Restore 2 changes). Regenerate the restored ops, then save the part. (1) Which load. (2) A value going back. (3) Values changed since – left alone. (4) Restore."},
{p:"undo-load.html",a:"example",t:"Undo a load",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"You loaded feed 0.012 into ops 7 and 8. After the load you also changed op 8's feed by hand in Mastercam to 0.011. Now the bore chatters and you want the old feeds back. Op Before the load Now Undo does After undo 7 feed 0.01 0.012 Restore 0.01 8 feed 0.01 0.011 Changed since the load – left alone 0.011 Run it a second time and it does nothing: the values are already back."},
{p:"undo-load.html",a:"messages",t:"Undo a load",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"There is no ParamTable.log beside ... so there is no load to undo. No load was made into this part from this folder, or the log was moved or deleted. ParamTable.log has no load of ... that can be undone. The log has no load of this part. Loads made by a much older version of the tool cannot be undone with this command; their old values are still in the log to put back by hand. Nothing to restore. / Already as before the load The values are already back (you ran undo before, or changed them back by hand). Restored 1 operation(s). They are marked for regeneration ... Done. Regenerate before posting, then save the part."},
{p:"undo-load.html",a:"related",t:"Undo a load",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Load the changes Undo inside Excel Troubleshooting"},
{p:"workbook.html",a:"what",t:"What is in the workbook",h:"What this is for",k:"workbook sheets tabs colours colors yellow blue grey ribbon Parameter Table tab xlsx xlsm macros ptconfig Dumped sheet",x:"A map of the workbook the dump makes: which sheet is which, what the colours mean, and where the buttons are. The sheet tabs are along the bottom of the Excel window."},
{p:"workbook.html",a:"when",t:"What is in the workbook",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"The first time you open a dumped workbook, or when a sheet or colour puzzles you."},
{p:"workbook.html",a:"steps",t:"What is in the workbook",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"The workbook opens on the Summary sheet: the part's totals. Click the Lathe params tab at the bottom: the main sheet, one row per op. This is where you edit. Click the Tools tab: one row per tool, with its picture and insert, and the inserts table under it. If you ticked Include macros, click the Parameter Table tab on Excel's ribbon (at the top) to see the tools below. The sheets Sheet What it holds You type in it? Summary Cycle time, flips, material removed – as dumped and now. A batch quantity and insert cost. The longest ops. Batch quantity only Lathe params One row per op: every setting the tool reads. Yes – the white cells Tools One row per tool; an inserts table under it. Insert names, edges, cost, edge life Dumped (hidden) A copy of every value as dumped. It is how changed cells light up, and how Back to the dump works. Never Changes Made by List changes. No Scenarios Made by Compare scenarios. No Program check Made by Program check. The Ignore column Slowest ops Made by Slowest ops. No Change report Made by Change report. The Why column The colours Looks like Means white You can type here. yellow You changed this cell. It differs from the dump. blue A worked-out number (time, flips, MRR) that moved because of your edits. grey Read-only, or the setting does not apply to this kind of op. Excel will not take typing here. blue line above and below a row The op row your cursor is on (with macros), so you do not lose your place on a wide sheet. The Parameter Table tab (with macros) FindGo toFind opNext matchChanged opsThis tool's opsSlowest opsShow all Edit cellsSetScale %Copy from op Selected opsComments & textCoolantSpeed & feedInspection & inserts ImproveHit a target timeProgram checkScenarios Undo and reviewUndoBack to the dumpList changesChange report HelpUser manual Every window these open has a ? button that opens its page in this manual. The .ptconfig file beside the part What you type on the Tools page and the Summary (insert names, edges, cost, parts per edge, edge life, batch quantity) is kept in a small file next to the part, called <part>.ptconfig. The next dump starts with those values, so you type them once. Delete the file to start over."},
{p:"workbook.html",a:"example",t:"What is in the workbook",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"You type 0.012 over the feed of op 7 (it was 0.01). Cell Before After op 7 feed 0.01 (white) 0.012 (yellow) op 7 changes 0 1 op 7 est_cycle_time 1:44:00 1:26:45 (blue) Summary: cycle time, Now 12:46:38 12:29:23 (1) Column group names. (2) Column names. (3) An edited cell (yellow). (4) The changes count for the row."},
{p:"workbook.html",a:"related",t:"What is in the workbook",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Summary page The main sheet Tools page Word list"},
{p:"summary.html",a:"",t:"Summary page",h:"",k:"summary totals cycle time cutting time flips per part batch quantity insert cost per batch longest operations air cutting removal rate",x:"Workbook: the Summary sheet (the first tab)"},
{p:"summary.html",a:"what",t:"Summary page",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"The Summary is the whole part on one page. It shows the totals as dumped and now, with the change. It follows every edit you make, as you type. Under that: a batch, the longest ops, the tools that flip inserts most, the ops that cut the most air, and how many inserts a part uses."},
{p:"summary.html",a:"when",t:"Summary page",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"Before you start: to see where the time goes. While you edit: to see the effect on the whole part. Before you load: to know what you will gain."},
{p:"summary.html",a:"steps",t:"Summary page",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Look at Part totals. As dumped is the program as it is in Mastercam. Now is with your edits. Change is the difference (minus = less). Type your batch quantity (how many parts in a run) in the box beside Batch quantity (type it in). The cycle time, flips and insert cost per batch follow. Type the edges and cost of each insert on the Tools page to get Insert cost per part and per batch. Look at Longest operations. These are where a change saves the most. Click an op number to jump to its row. Look at Insert flips by tool and Most air cutting for more ideas. (1) Cycle time as dumped and now. (2) Type the batch quantity here. (3) Insert cost for the batch (whole inserts). (1) Click an op number to go to its row. (2) Its share of the whole part's time."},
{p:"summary.html",a:"example",t:"Summary page",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"Oil Spool Head, after raising the bore feeds (ops 7 and 8) to 0.012: What As dumped Now Change Cycle time (estimate) 12:46:38 12:26:50 -0:19:49 Cutting time 12:42:43 12:22:54 -0:19:49 Insert flips per part 89 89 0 Average removal rate (per min) 1.74 1.79 0.05 The prime turning rough (op 2) is 72.8% of the part. That is where the next big saving is."},
{p:"summary.html",a:"messages",t:"Summary page",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"No estimated times - regenerate and dump again. The ops had no toolpath when dumped (they needed regenerating). Regenerate in Mastercam, then dump again. No insert flips counted on this part. No op has tool inspection stops that change the insert. See How insert flips are counted. Insert cost is blank Type the edges and the cost per insert on the Tools page."},
{p:"summary.html",a:"related",t:"Summary page",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Slowest ops Tools page How the cycle time estimate works MRR and material removed"},
{p:"sheet.html",a:"",t:"The main sheet",h:"",k:"Lathe params main sheet row column group fold unfold plus minus outline dropdown tooltip hover read-only grey empty cell edit type",x:"Workbook: the Lathe params sheet"},
{p:"sheet.html",a:"what",t:"The main sheet",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"This is where you edit. Each row is one op, in the same order as Mastercam's Operation Manager. Row 1 has the group names (Feeds and speeds, Coolant ...). Row 2 has the column names (feed, speed ...). The first columns – op number, type, tool, comment – stay in view when you scroll right. The column groups. The ones you change most are first."},
{p:"sheet.html",a:"when",t:"The main sheet",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"Any time you change a setting. You can type straight into the cells, like any spreadsheet. The ribbon tools (Set, Scale ...) just make big changes quicker."},
{p:"sheet.html",a:"steps",t:"The main sheet",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Find the op's row. Scroll, or use Find op on the ribbon. Find the column. Hover over a column name in row 2 to read what it is. A group that is folded away shows only one column: click the small + above the column letters to open it. (With macros: Go to on the ribbon opens a group for you.) Type the new value and press Enter. The cell turns yellow. Click a cell with a small arrow on its right to pick from a list (for example per rev / per min). Watch the row's numbers move: est_cycle_time, flips and MRR follow your edit. See How the estimate works. Save when you are done (Ctrl+S). Rules that keep you safe An empty cell means “leave it alone” – never zero. Each cell checks what you type. A value Mastercam would not take is refused straight away, with a message saying what the cell takes. Click Retry or Cancel. Grey cells refuse typing. They are read-only (a time, a count) or the setting is not part of this kind of op. A feed is two cells: the number (feed, always positive) and its unit (feed_mode: per rev or per min). A speed is the number (speed), CSS or RPM (speed_mode) and CW or CCW (spindle_dir). Sorting and filtering are fine. The load finds each row by its op number, not by where it is. X values are a radius in the travel columns (double them for diameter). A few angles are in radians – the tooltip says so."},
{p:"sheet.html",a:"example",t:"The main sheet",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"Op 17 (Multiple Plunge Groove) plunges at 0.0025 inch per rev. You want 0.003. Find op 17's row. Click its feed cell (Feeds and speeds group). Type 0.003, press Enter. op 17 Before After feed 0.0025 0.003 feed_mode per rev per rev changes 0 1 est_cycle_time 0:09:00 shorter (blue) If you had typed -0.003 by mistake, the cell would refuse it: a feed is never below 0 here. (1) A group name. (2) A column name – hover it for help. (3) An edited cell. (4) The row's changes count."},
{p:"sheet.html",a:"messages",t:"The main sheet",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"Excel: This value doesn't match the data validation restrictions ... (or the cell's own message) The value is outside what the setting takes. The message says what it takes, e.g. A number, 0 or more. The cell will not take any typing It is grey: read-only, or not for this kind of op. Its message says which. Status bar: op 7: CSS with no max_ss. or op 7: feed 0.01 per min is very slow - meant per rev? A gentle warning, not a refusal. CSS with no max RPM can spin very fast near the center. A per-minute feed of 0.01 is probably a per-rev number in the wrong mode (and a per-rev feed over 0.1 in is probably a per-minute one). Check the row."},
{p:"sheet.html",a:"related",t:"The main sheet",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Word list – every column in plain words Find an op and Go to a column group Undo How the cycle time estimate works"},
{p:"tools-page.html",a:"",t:"Tools page",h:"",k:"tools page tool list picture insert edges per insert cost per insert parts per edge edge life fallback usual edge time flips per part cut time inserts table",x:"Workbook: the Tools sheet"},
{p:"tools-page.html",a:"what",t:"Tools page",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"One row per tool: its picture, which ops use it, its insert, how many insert flips a part takes, and how long it cuts. Under the tools is the inserts table: one row per insert, with its edges and cost. From those, the workbook works out how many inserts a part uses and what they cost. In the main sheet, click a tool number to jump here."},
{p:"tools-page.html",a:"when",t:"Tools page",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"To see which tools eat inserts. To get the insert cost per part and per batch (type edges and cost once – they are kept for next time). When the insert name the tool guessed is wrong."},
{p:"tools-page.html",a:"steps",t:"Tools page",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Check each tool's insert (the Insert column). The tool works it out from the tool's shape, e.g. C 80° r0.031. If it is wrong, type over it. Tools with the same insert name are added up together below. Look at the flips: Flips / part, Cut time / part, Longest between flips, and Inspection (what its ops stop on). Edge life (fallback): only for ops that stop for inspection with no time of their own. Type the cut time one edge lasts, as minutes:seconds (8:00). Greyed out where no op uses it. Insert size (IC) and Entering angle (at the right end of each tool row): the insert's size across its inscribed circle (a round insert's diameter), and the holder's entering angle – read from its ISO code (PCLNL: an L-style holder, 95°; 90° = square shoulder). The chip thinning calculator uses them. Type over a wrong one; it is kept. Leave the angle blank for round, grooving and PrimeTurning tools. In the inserts table, type the edges per insert (a CNMG: 4; a VNMG: 4; a VCMT: 2; a round: about 8). The guess counts one side only – type over it for a double-sided insert. Type the cost per insert. Inserts / part and Insert cost / part fill in. The Summary page takes them to a batch. Parts per edge – only for an insert that lasts more than one part (the toolpath never flips it). Type how many parts one edge makes. It is greyed out where the toolpath already flips the edge every part. Usual edge time (the last column of the inserts table): how long one edge of this insert usually cuts in your shop before it is turned, as minutes:seconds (12:00). The Program check warns about a tool whose edge runs more than 25% longer. Leave it blank and the check compares the tools in this program with each other instead. (1) The insert – type over it if it is wrong. (2) Flips per part. (3) Edge life, only where it is used. (1) Edges per insert. (2) Cost per insert. (3) Parts per edge (only for inserts that outlast a part)."},
{p:"tools-page.html",a:"example",t:"Tools page",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"On the Oil Spool Head, tools T11 and T12 both carry a C 80° r0.031 insert. Together they flip 15 edges per part. The guess says 2 edges, but it is a CNMG – double-sided, 4 edges. Inserts cost $12. C 80° r0.031 Before After Edges per insert 2 4 Flips / part 15 15 Inserts / part 8 4 Cost per insert (blank) $12.00 Insert cost / part (blank) $45.00 15 flips ÷ 4 edges = 3.75 inserts, shown as 4 whole inserts. The cost uses 3.75 × $12 = $45.00. Next dump, the 4 and the $12 are already there (kept in the part's .ptconfig file)."},
{p:"tools-page.html",a:"messages",t:"Tools page",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"A whole number of edges, 1 or more. Edges per insert takes a whole number. Type a time: minutes:seconds (8:00) or seconds (480). Edge life and usual edge time take a time. 8:00 and 480 mean the same. A size more than 0, in the part's units - or blank. / An angle in degrees, more than 0 and less than 180 - or blank. Insert size (IC) and Entering angle take a number, or nothing. A number of parts, more than 0 - and only for an insert whose edge is not already flipped every part ... Parts per edge is only for inserts the toolpath never flips. Leave it blank for the others. no insert data - grouped by its cutting outline (Shape check column) The tool in Mastercam has no insert data, so the name was worked out from its outline. Check the name."},
{p:"tools-page.html",a:"related",t:"Tools page",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Inspection & inserts – use every edge evenly How insert flips are counted Summary page (insert cost per batch) Edge life"},
{p:"find.html",a:"",t:"Find an op",h:"",k:"find op search jump go to column group next match changed ops this tool's ops show all filter hide rows row mark lost my place Find an op fast By op number, tool (T12) or a word in the comment.",x:"Ribbon: Parameter Table tab › Find"},
{p:"find.html",a:"what",t:"Find an op",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"A part can have dozens of ops and hundreds of columns. The Find group gets you to the right row and the right columns fast – and can hide the rows you do not need right now."},
{p:"find.html",a:"when",t:"Find an op",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"“Where is op 23?” – or “where are all the ops of tool 12?” “Where are the coolant columns?” “Which ops did I change?”"},
{p:"find.html",a:"steps",t:"Find an op",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Find an op by number, tool or words Click in the Find op box on the ribbon. Type one of these, then press Enter: an op number: 23 a T and a tool number: T12 words from the comment or the type: bore, groove The sheet jumps to the first op that matches. The status bar (bottom of Excel) names it. Click Next match for the next one. After the last, it starts again at the top. Go to a column group Select a cell in the op rows you care about (or just one). Click Go to and pick a group, e.g. Coolant. That group opens and the others fold away, so the sheet is easy to read. The cursor lands in that group, on the same rows. To open every group again: Go to › All columns (open every group). Show only some ops Changed ops – only the ops you have changed. This tool's ops – click a cell in an op's row first; shows only the ops with the same tool. Slowest ops – only the ops that take most of the time. Show all – every op again. Hidden rows are not lost. The tools that work on “selected cells” skip hidden rows, so you can show only one tool's ops, select a whole column, and change just those. The row mark. The op row your cursor is on gets a blue line above and below (and its first columns a light blue tint). On a wide sheet it stops you reading across into the wrong row. (1) Go to. (2) Find op. (3) Next match. (4) Show only some ops."},
{p:"find.html",a:"example",t:"Find an op",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"You want every op of tool 12 on the Oil Spool Head, with their feeds. Type T12 in Find op, press Enter: the cursor goes to op 7. Click This tool's ops. Click Go to › Feeds and speeds. Before After Rows showing 18 ops 2 ops (7 and 8) Column groups open all Identity and Feeds and speeds Cursor op 1 op 7, feed Click Show all and Go to › All columns when you are done."},
{p:"find.html",a:"messages",t:"Find an op",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"no op matches 'xyz' (an op number, T and a tool number, or words in the comment). Nothing matched. Check the spelling, or try fewer words. Hidden rows are not searched – click Show all first. type an op number, T12 or words from a comment in Find op first. You clicked Next match before searching. Click a cell in an operation's row (one with a tool number) first. This tool's ops needs to know which tool: click a cell in that op's row."},
{p:"find.html",a:"related",t:"Find an op",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Slowest ops The main sheet (column groups) Set – change the cells you found"},
{p:"slowest.html",a:"",t:"Slowest ops",h:"",k:"slowest ops longest operations where the time goes 80% cycle time faster bottleneck sort by time Make the part run faster Find the slow ops, then speed them up.",x:"Ribbon: Parameter Table tab › Find › Slowest ops"},
{p:"slowest.html",a:"what",t:"Slowest ops",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"On most parts, a few ops take most of the time. Slowest ops shows only the ops that make up 80% of the cycle time and hides the rest. It also makes a Slowest ops sheet that ranks them, longest first. That is where a change saves the most."},
{p:"slowest.html",a:"when",t:"Slowest ops",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"First thing, whenever the goal is “make it faster”. Saving 10% on an op that is 70% of the part beats saving 50% on an op that is 1% of it."},
{p:"slowest.html",a:"steps",t:"Slowest ops",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Click Slowest ops. The Lathe params sheet now shows only the slow ops (in their usual order – the rows are hidden, not moved). The status bar at the bottom says how many, e.g. 2 of 18 ops make 80% of the cycle time. The Slowest ops sheet lists them longest first: Time, Share of the part, Running share (adding up), Cut time and Air time. Click an op to go to its row. Change them (type, Scale %, or Hit a target time). Click Show all on the ribbon to see every op again. The Slowest ops sheet. Running share adds up the shares, longest first."},
{p:"slowest.html",a:"example",t:"Slowest ops",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"The Oil Spool Head, after the bore feeds went to 0.012, runs 12:26:50. Slowest ops shows: Op Time Share Running share Air time 2 Prime Turn Rough OD (T1) 9:04:03 72.8% 72.8% 30:19 7 Rough Bore ID (T12) 1:26:45 11.6% 84.5% 14:14 Two ops out of 18 are 84.5% of the time. Everything else together is 15.5%. Start with op 2 – and look at its half hour of air cutting."},
{p:"slowest.html",a:"messages",t:"Slowest ops",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"2 of 18 ops make 80% of the 12:26:50 cycle time ... How many ops are left showing, and the part's live cycle time. No op has an estimated time - regenerate the ops and dump again. The ops had no toolpath when dumped. Regenerate in Mastercam and dump again."},
{p:"slowest.html",a:"related",t:"Slowest ops",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Summary page – the ten longest ops Hit a target time Show all"},
{p:"set.html",a:"",t:"Set",h:"",k:"set selected cells same value fill many cells bulk edit all at once feed depth stock Put one value in many cells Same feed, same depth, same anything.",x:"Ribbon: Parameter Table tab › Edit cells › Set"},
{p:"set.html",a:"what",t:"Set",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"Puts one value into every cell you selected. Before anything is written, the window tells you how many cells will change and which ones are refused (read-only, not for that kind of op, or outside its limits)."},
{p:"set.html",a:"when",t:"Set",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"The same feed, depth or stock to leave on many ops. The same choice on many ops: per rev, CSS, a yes/no switch. For coolant, use Coolant (Set on coolant cells opens it for you). For a percent up or down, use Scale %."},
{p:"set.html",a:"steps",t:"Set",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Select the cells on the Lathe params sheet. To pick cells that are not next to each other, hold Ctrl while you click. To pick a whole column of ops, click the first cell, hold Shift, click the last. Click Set on the ribbon. Type the value (or pick it from the list). The list shows what is in the cells now, or the cell's own choices. Read the line under it: how many cells will change, how many already have that value, how many are refused. The list below shows each cell, old → new. Click OK. The cells turn yellow. The status bar (bottom of Excel) says what was done. (1) Type or pick the value. (2) What OK will do. (3) Each cell, old → new. (4) OK. Hidden rows are skipped. Use This tool's ops or Changed ops first, then select the whole column – only the rows you can see are set."},
{p:"set.html",a:"example",t:"Set",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"Set the feed of ops 1, 7 and 8 to 0.012 per rev. Click op 1's feed cell. Hold Ctrl and click op 7's and op 8's. Click Set, type 0.012. The window says 3 cell(s) will change. Click OK. Op feed before feed after 1 Face Stock 0.01 0.012 7 Rough Bore ID 0.01 0.012 8 Finish Bore ID 0.01 0.012 Changed your mind? Press Ctrl+Z (or Undo) – all three go back at once."},
{p:"set.html",a:"messages",t:"Set",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"3 cell(s) will change, 1 already that. One of the cells already holds that value; it is left as is. ... 2 refused (read-only, not for that operation, or outside its limits). Those cells cannot take that value. They stay as they are. The list shows which. Type or pick a value. The box is empty – OK stays grey until you type something. Select cells on the 'Lathe params' sheet first. You were on another sheet. Go to Lathe params and select cells."},
{p:"set.html",a:"related",t:"Set",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Scale % Copy from op Undo"},
{p:"scale.html",a:"",t:"Scale %",h:"",k:"scale percent percentage increase decrease bigger smaller 10% more less multiply feed speed override Make numbers 10% bigger or smaller Scale feeds, speeds or depths by a percent.",x:"Ribbon: Parameter Table tab › Edit cells › Scale %"},
{p:"scale.html",a:"what",t:"Scale %",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"Makes every selected number bigger or smaller by the same percent – like turning the feed override knob, but saved in the program. Each cell keeps its own value: 0.010 and 0.020 at +10% become 0.011 and 0.022."},
{p:"scale.html",a:"when",t:"Scale %",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"“These ops can all go 10% faster.” “Back the speeds off 15% on this tough material.” To reach a set cycle time instead, use Hit a target time."},
{p:"scale.html",a:"steps",t:"Scale %",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Select the number cells to change (e.g. the feed of several ops). Text cells and worked-out cells are skipped. Click Scale %. Type the change, with its sign: +10% means 10% more, -10% means 10% less. (Or pick one from the list.) A bare 10% is refused – the window asks which way. Read the preview: how many cells change, and each cell, old → new. Cells that would go past their limits are refused and listed. Click OK. Ctrl+Z takes it back. (1) The change, with its sign. (2) What OK will do. (3) OK."},
{p:"scale.html",a:"example",t:"Scale %",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"Raise the feeds of the facing op and the two bore ops by 10%. Select the feed cells of ops 1, 7 and 8 (hold Ctrl), click Scale %, type +10%, click OK. Op feed before +10% 1 Face Stock 0.01 0.011 7 Rough Bore ID 0.01 0.011 8 Finish Bore ID 0.01 0.011 The estimate on each row drops by about 9% of its cutting time (time goes down as feed goes up: 1 ÷ 1.10 = 0.91)."},
{p:"scale.html",a:"messages",t:"Scale %",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"10% more: 3 cell(s) will change. What OK will do. The list below shows each cell, old → new. '10%' - say which way: +10% for more, -10% for less. Type the sign. Type +10% for 10% more, or -10% for 10% less. / ... is not a percent - type +10% or -10%. The box is empty, or not a percent. -100% would leave nothing - type a smaller cut, such as -10%. A cut of 100% or more would make the values zero. ... refused (read-only, not for that operation, or outside its limits) The new value would be past what the setting takes. Those cells are left as they are."},
{p:"scale.html",a:"related",t:"Scale %",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Hit a target time Set Undo What +10% means"},
{p:"copy.html",a:"",t:"Copy from op",h:"",k:"copy from op paste match same as to all ops of tool every op tool settings feed speed copy across Make ops match each other Copy one op's settings to others, or to every op of a tool.",x:"Ribbon: Parameter Table tab › Edit cells › Copy from op"},
{p:"copy.html",a:"what",t:"Copy from op",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"Makes ops match. You pick the columns (by selecting cells in them), pick the op to copy from, and the values go into the other ops you selected – or into every op that uses the same tool."},
{p:"copy.html",a:"when",t:"Copy from op",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"One op runs well. You want the same feed and speed on a similar op. You tuned one op of a tool and want all of that tool's ops the same."},
{p:"copy.html",a:"steps",t:"Copy from op",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Click the op to copy from – in a column to copy – so the cursor is on it. Select the cells to copy (for example feed and speed). Hold Ctrl to add the same cells in the rows to copy into. Click Copy from op. Copy from: starts on the op the cursor is on. Pick another in the list if you like (it shows each op's number, type and comment). Into: The selected rows, or Every other op of tool ... (every op with the same tool as the op you copy from). Read the preview: how many cells change, and each cell, old → new. Click OK. Ctrl+Z takes it back. Units are checked. A feed is not copied into a row whose feed is in other units (per rev / per min), and a speed not into a row with the other of CSS / RPM – those cells are refused and listed. Copy the unit column too (feed_mode, speed_mode) and they go across together. A feed of 0.012 per minute would be a crawl. (1) Copy from. (2) Into. (3) What OK will do. (4) OK."},
{p:"copy.html",a:"example",t:"Copy from op",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"Op 7 (Rough Bore ID) now runs well at 0.012 per rev and 220 SFM. Make op 8 (Finish Bore ID, same tool T12) the same. Click op 7's feed cell, drag across to its speed cell. Hold Ctrl and do the same on op 8. Click Copy from op. Copy from shows op 7, Into shows The selected rows: op 8. The window says 2 cell(s) will change. Click OK. op 8 Before After feed 0.01 0.012 speed 200 220 feed_mode, speed_mode per rev, CSS per rev, CSS (same – no warning) A finish pass often wants a lighter feed than the rough. Copy what makes sense, not everything."},
{p:"copy.html",a:"messages",t:"Copy from op",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"Pick the op to copy from. No source op is picked yet. The selected rows - none besides op 7 (select the rows to copy into) Only the source op is selected. Hold Ctrl and add cells in the rows to copy into, or pick Every other op of tool .... No other op uses this tool - pick the selected rows, or select some. Every other op of tool found only the op you copy from. ... not copied - the units differ (per rev / per min, CSS / RPM); listed below See the box above: copy the unit column too. ... refused (read-only, not for that operation, or outside its limits) That column does not exist for the target's kind of op, or the value is past its limits."},
{p:"copy.html",a:"related",t:"Copy from op",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Set This tool's ops Per rev and per min, CSS and RPM"},
{p:"text.html",a:"",t:"Comments & text",h:"",k:"comment op comment inspection comment insp_comment ROTATE INSERT CHANGE INSERT INDEX INSERT CHECK INSERT manual entry text manual_text characters limit 119 double-click Change a comment or inspection comment Op comments, ROTATE INSERT, manual text.",x:"Ribbon: Parameter Table tab › Selected ops › Comments & text"},
{p:"text.html",a:"what",t:"Comments & text",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"A roomy window for the three kinds of text an op has: Op comment – the name the Operation Manager shows (comment, up to 119 characters). Inspection comment – the message at a tool inspection stop (insp_comment), e.g. ROTATE INSERT. Its limit is the cell's own (often 49 characters). Manual text – the text of a manual entry op (manual_text, up to 3,111 characters). Only for manual entry ops, one op at a time. It counts the characters as you type, against each one's limit. With several ops selected, what you type goes on all of them."},
{p:"text.html",a:"when",t:"Comments & text",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"To rename ops so the setup sheet and the operator make sense of them. To tell the operator what to do at an inspection stop (rotate the insert, check a size). To edit a long manual entry without squinting at a cell."},
{p:"text.html",a:"steps",t:"Comments & text",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Select the ops – any cell in their rows. (Or double-click a comment, insp_comment or manual_text cell: the window opens on that tab.) Click Comments & text. The line at the top says which ops it is on. Pick the tab: Op comment, Inspection comment or Manual text. A tab the ops do not have is greyed out. Type the text. The count under the box shows the characters against the limit. If the ops have different text now, it says Mixed - these 2 ops have different comments now; what you type goes on all of them. On the Inspection comment tab you can click a common comment instead: ROTATE INSERT, CHANGE INSERT, INDEX INSERT, CHECK INSERT. If a stop's comment is switched off, tick Also switch the comment on at the stop. Manual text: pick the op in the Op list (one op at a time). Ctrl+Enter applies. Click Apply. The cells turn yellow. Ctrl+Z takes the whole Apply back. The window stays open beside the sheet. Click other ops and it follows them – unless you have typed something you have not applied yet (then it waits: Apply, or Revert to follow the sheet again). Close or Esc throws away typing you have not applied. < Prev and Next > move to the op above or below. The ? button opens this page. The words in an inspection comment matter. A stop whose comment says ROTATE, FLIP, CHANGE or INDEX is counted as an insert flip – a new edge. CHECK INSERT is only a look, not a flip. Change CHECK INSERT to ROTATE INSERT and the flips per part go up. (1) The ops it is on. (2) The three tabs. (3) Apply – nothing is written before it."},
{p:"text.html",a:"example",t:"Comments & text",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"Say your rough bore and finish bore (ops 7 and 8, tool T12) stop for inspection with the comment CHECK INSERT. In your shop the operator always turns the insert at that stop. Say so in the comment, and the flips will be counted. Click a cell in op 7's row, hold Ctrl, click a cell in op 8's row. Click Comments & text, then the Inspection comment tab. Click ROTATE INSERT, then Apply. Op insp_comment before insp_comment after Stops counted as flips (after load and regenerate) 7 Rough Bore ID CHECK INSERT ROTATE INSERT no → yes 8 Finish Bore ID CHECK INSERT ROTATE INSERT no → yes After you load and regenerate, the NC code carries the message at each stop. If the stop's comment was switched off, tick Also switch the comment on at the stop before Apply."},
{p:"text.html",a:"messages",t:"Comments & text",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"... over the limit by 12 / The op comment is longer than 119 characters. Too long. Shorten it – Apply waits, and the load would refuse text that is cut off. Mixed - these 2 ops have different comments now. What you type goes on all of them. The selected ops have different text. Whatever you type replaces all of them. None of these ops is a manual entry - no manual text. / None of these ops has a tool inspection comment. That tab does not apply to the ops you picked. Not applied yet - Apply writes it to the sheet (Ctrl+Z takes it back). You typed something. Click Apply to keep it, or Revert to drop it. Apply or Revert first - then move to another op. You pressed Prev / Next with typing not applied. op 8 inspection comment: longer than its 49 characters After Apply: that cell was refused. The other ops were written."},
{p:"text.html",a:"related",t:"Comments & text",h:"Related",k:"",x:"How insert flips are counted Inspection & inserts Program check (finds ops with no comment)"},
{p:"coolant.html",a:"",t:"Coolant",h:"",k:"coolant flood mist thru-tool through spindle high pressure off M08 M09 coolant_before coolant_with coolant_after V9 X-style mixed Change coolant on several ops Flood, mist, thru-tool or off – all at once.",x:"Ribbon: Parameter Table tab › Selected ops › Coolant"},
{p:"coolant.html",a:"what",t:"Coolant",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"Shows the coolant of the ops you selected and sets it for all of them at once. It works for both ways a Mastercam machine can be set up for coolant: One setting per op (“V9” coolant): Off, Flood, Mist or Thru-tool. Column coolant. Before / with / after the move (“X-style” coolant), using your machine's own coolant names. Columns coolant_before, coolant_with, coolant_after. Each can hold more than one coolant. Each op follows its own machine, so one part can have both kinds."},
{p:"coolant.html",a:"when",t:"Coolant",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"A program came over with coolant off on cutting ops. You want thru-tool on all the drills, or mist instead of flood on a whole group."},
{p:"coolant.html",a:"steps",t:"Coolant",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Select the ops – any cell in their rows. (Or select coolant cells and click Set: it opens this window too.) Click Coolant. The sheet jumps to the coolant columns, so you see what is set now. Read what is set now. If the ops differ, it says how, e.g. Mixed: Flood x2, Off x1. Pick the coolant. One setting (V9): pick Off, Flood, Mist or Thru-tool for all of these ops. Before / with / after the move (X-style): a grid of your machine's coolants. Tick the ones to turn on. A grey box means some of the ops have it and some do not – leave it grey and each op keeps its own. If you selected ops of both kinds, the window shows both parts. Click Apply. Only what you changed is written. Ops it could not set are listed, with the reason. The window stays open beside the sheet and follows the ops you click (unless you have changed something you have not applied). Ctrl+Z takes back the whole Apply. (1) The ops it is on, and what they have now. (2) Apply."},
{p:"coolant.html",a:"example",t:"Coolant",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"On the Oil Spool Head, the prime turning ops (2, 3, 4, 5, 6) run with coolant Off. The others run Flood. You want flood on the two roughs, ops 2 and 3. Select a cell in op 2's row and op 3's row. Click Coolant. It shows Now: Off (both the same). Pick Flood, click Apply. Op coolant before coolant after 2 Prime Turn Rough OD Off Flood 3 Prime Turn Rest Rough OD Off Flood Coolant does not change the cycle time estimate. It is listed in the load preview like any other change."},
{p:"coolant.html",a:"messages",t:"Coolant",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"Mixed: Flood x2, Off x1 Two of the selected ops have Flood and one has Off. Pick one to set them all the same – or, in the grid, leave the box grey to keep each op's own. op 7 (with the move): this machine has no Thru-tool That op's machine does not have that coolant, so the op was left as it was. Each machine has its own list. Nothing to set - these ops have no coolant. Some ops have no coolant setting at all (a transform, a manual entry). More coolants than shown here ... The machine has more coolants than the window has room for. Set the rest in the cells. Not applied yet - Apply writes it to the sheet (Ctrl+Z takes it back). You picked something. Apply it, or Revert to drop it."},
{p:"coolant.html",a:"related",t:"Coolant",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Program check (finds cutting ops with coolant off) Set Coolant words"},
{p:"calculator.html",a:"",t:"Speed & feed",h:"",k:"calculator speed feed SFM RPM surface speed spindle speed m/min per rev per minute IPR IPM diameter max RPM chip thinning chip thickness round insert lead angle entering angle dynamic turning Work out SFM, RPM or a feed Speed & feed, and chip thinning.",x:"Ribbon: Parameter Table tab › Selected ops › Speed & feed"},
{p:"calculator.html",a:"what",t:"Speed & feed",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"A calculator that fills itself in from the op you are on. It has two tabs: Speed & feed – SFM and RPM at a diameter, and feed per rev and per minute. Type in any box; the others follow. It warns when the RPM would go past the op's max RPM. Chip thinning – how thick the chip really is with a round insert or an insert with a lead angle, and the feed to program for the chip you want. It can write that feed back to the op."},
{p:"calculator.html",a:"when",t:"Speed & feed",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"The insert box says 650 SFM and you want to know the RPM at your diameter. You need the feed in inches per minute for a per-minute op. A round insert or a 45° lead insert is rubbing instead of cutting – the chip is thinner than the feed."},
{p:"calculator.html",a:"steps",t:"Speed & feed",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Speed & feed tab Click a cell in the op's row, then click Speed & feed. (Or pick the op in the window's op list, or use < / > for the op above or below.) The boxes fill in from the op, in the op's own units (inch or mm): Diameter (the op's average cutting diameter, cut_dia, when it is known), Surface speed (SFM), Spindle speed (RPM), Feed per rev, Feed per minute. Type a new value in any box. The others work it out: RPM = SFM × 3.82 ÷ diameter (inch), feed per minute = feed per rev × RPM. Watch the note under the boxes: above the op's max RPM (max_ss) the spindle stops at the max, so the real surface speed is lower – and the feed per minute is worked out at the max RPM. To use a new feed, click the button under the boxes – it says what it will do, e.g. Set this op's feed: 0.01 -> 0.012. The cell turns yellow; Ctrl+Z takes it back. Chip thinning tab Click the Chip thinning tab. The line at the top says the tool and its insert. Pick the insert kind if it is not right: a round insert (type its size and the depth of cut) or a straight edge (type the holder's Entering angle – or the US Lead angle, which is 90° minus it; type either one). Read the chip at today's feed: real chip = feed × sin(entering angle). A round insert's angle comes from its size and the depth: the shallower the cut, the thinner the chip. Type the chip thickness you want. It shows the feed per rev to program. Click Set this op's feed to write it (as a per-minute feed if the op runs per minute). The insert size and entering angle come from the Tools page columns Insert size (IC) and Entering angle. The angle is read from the holder's ISO code (PCLNL: an L-style holder, 95°). Type over either one there if it is wrong – it is remembered. PrimeTurning, grooving, mill and drill ops get a short explanation instead of a guess. The window stays open beside the sheet and follows the op you click. (1) The op it is on – pick another here. (2) The two tabs. The Chip thinning tab (1), with an entering angle of 45° typed: a 0.01 feed makes a 0.00707 chip. (This sample's holder is really a 95° one, which barely thins the chip.)"},
{p:"calculator.html",a:"example",t:"Speed & feed",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"Speed and feed Op 7 (Rough Bore ID) cuts at about 6.905″ diameter, 200 SFM, 0.010 per rev. Before You type 260 SFM Surface speed (SFM) 200 260 RPM at 6.905″ 110.6 143.8 Feed per rev 0.010 0.010 Feed per minute 1.11 1.44 Chip thinning A 0.5″ round insert cutting 0.050″ deep. Its entering angle works out to about 37° (sin = 0.6), so the chip is only 60% of the feed. Programmed now For a 0.006″ chip Feed per rev 0.006 0.010 Real chip 0.0036 0.006 A 45° lead insert is the same idea: sin 45° = 0.71, so a 0.010 feed gives a 0.0071 chip."},
{p:"calculator.html",a:"messages",t:"Speed & feed",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"Above this op's max spindle speed of 3500 RPM: at this diameter the spindle stops at ... The RPM you asked for is more than the op allows. The machine runs at the max, so the surface speed is lower than you typed. This op's speed is CSS with no max spindle speed. Near the center the spindle could run to the machine's top speed. Set a max RPM. At half the insert or deeper the chip is as thick as the feed - no thinning. A round insert cutting half its size deep (or more) has no chip thinning. Program the feed you want as the chip. Type the insert diameter and the depth of cut. / Type the holder's entering angle ... - or its US lead angle. The Tools page has no size or angle for this tool. Type it here (and on the Tools page, to keep it). PrimeTurning ops set their own feeds from a chip thickness (pt_rough_chip) - not worked out here. Change a PrimeTurning op's chip in its own columns (pt_rough_chip ...). op 7 feed 0.01 -> 0.012 per rev - Ctrl+Z to undo The feed was written to the sheet. Refused - op 7 feed: ... is outside this feed's limits, or this op takes no feed. The feed it worked out is past what the op takes. Nothing was written."},
{p:"calculator.html",a:"related",t:"Speed & feed",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Chip thinning, entering angle CSS and max RPM How the cycle time estimate works"},
{p:"inspection.html",a:"",t:"Inspection & inserts",h:"",k:"inspection inserts insert flips per part edge time edge life insp_time tool life even out flips fewer inserts insert cost wasted edge last edge used suggested Use fewer inserts / lower insert cost Flips per part and how much of each edge gets used.",x:"Ribbon: Parameter Table tab › Selected ops › Inspection & inserts"},
{p:"inspection.html",a:"what",t:"Inspection & inserts",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"Shows, for each tool, how often it stops to turn its insert and how much of each edge gets used. Often the last edge of a part is thrown away half used. The window works out an edge time (insp_time) that splits the tool's cutting evenly over its edges – fewer flips, no wasted edge – and writes it to every op of the tool."},
{p:"inspection.html",a:"when",t:"Inspection & inserts",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"Insert cost per part is high (see the Summary). A tool flips one more time per part than it needs to. After you changed feeds or speeds – the cutting time changed, so the best edge time did too."},
{p:"inspection.html",a:"steps",t:"Inspection & inserts",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Select the ops of the tools to look at (any cell in their rows), then click Inspection & inserts. (Tools: can also show every tool on the sheet.) Pick the goal: Same flips - use every edge evenly (the start), One flip fewer per part, or One flip more per part. Read one line per tool: Insert, Flips / part, Edge time (how long each edge cuts now), Last edge (how much of that the last edge in a part gets to cut), New edge time, New flips and a Note. Click a tool to read its whole note. Tick the tools to change. Check the note: Edge runs 0:05 longer than now - check the insert can take it means each edge would cut longer. Click Apply. The new edge time goes into insp_time on every op of each ticked tool that times its stops (yellow). The flips on the sheet and the Summary follow. The goal goes back to Same flips. “Change them in Mastercam.” Some tools stop for another reason – after a number of cuts, by distance, or at the end of the op. A new edge time does not move those stops, so the tool cannot be ticked. Its note says why, e.g. Flips come from stops in the toolpath (every 5 in of cut) - change them in Mastercam. Change the op's Tool inspection settings in Mastercam instead. (1) The goal. (2) One line per tool – tick the ones to change. (3) Apply."},
{p:"inspection.html",a:"example",t:"Inspection & inserts",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"On the Oil Spool Head, tool T4 (the PrimeTurning rest rough, op 3) turns its insert every 1:10 of cutting – 12 flips per part – and the last edge of a part cuts only 63% of that. Goal: One flip fewer per part. T4 Now After Apply Edge time (insp_time on op 3) 1:10 1:15 Flips per part 12 11 Whole part, flips per part 89 88 Note Edge runs 0:05 longer than now – check the insert can take it. Each edge cuts 5 seconds longer, and every part saves one edge. Tools T11, T1 and T12 cannot be ticked: their flips come from stops in the toolpath (every so many cuts, every 5 or 10 in of cut). If the new edge time is much longer than what the insert really lasts, do not use it – look at the feeds, or a tougher grade, instead."},
{p:"inspection.html",a:"messages",t:"Inspection & inserts",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"Flips come from stops in the toolpath (every 5 in of cut, at the end) - change them in Mastercam. The tool's flips do not come from an edge time. See the note above. Already even - every edge cuts about the same. Nothing to gain with Same flips. Try One flip fewer per part. Edge runs 0:05 longer than now - check the insert can take it. The new edge time is longer than today's. Fine if the insert lasts; risky if it already wears out. Same flips - each edge cuts 1:45 less. The edges are used more evenly for the same flips: easier on the inserts. Nothing to change - every tool here is even already, or its flips come from the toolpath. No tool can be improved with an edge time. Done: edge time set on op 3 ... Applied. Ctrl+Z takes it back. A tool shows 0 flips Its ops do not stop for inspection, or the stops are not insert flips (their comment does not say ROTATE, FLIP, CHANGE or INDEX). See How insert flips are counted."},
{p:"inspection.html",a:"related",t:"Inspection & inserts",h:"Related",k:"",x:"How insert flips are counted Tools page (edges and cost per insert) Comments & text (inspection comments) Edge life"},
{p:"target-time.html",a:"",t:"Hit a target time",h:"",k:"target time cycle time goal faster reduce time quote hit a time percent faster feeds factor limits speeds too main feed only Hit a cycle time I was given Type the time; the feeds follow.",x:"Ribbon: Parameter Table tab › Improve › Hit a target time"},
{p:"target-time.html",a:"what",t:"Hit a target time",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"You say how long some ops should take. The tool finds one factor for their feeds that gets there – each feed kept inside its own limits – and shows every op before and after: time, flips and MRR. Nothing is written until you click Apply."},
{p:"target-time.html",a:"when",t:"Hit a target time",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"A quote or a schedule needs the part (or a tool's ops) under a certain time. “Take 10% off this tool's time” – and you want to see what that does to the inserts first."},
{p:"target-time.html",a:"steps",t:"Hit a target time",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Select the ops (any cell in their rows), or click a cell in one op of a tool. Click Hit a target time. The window opens beside the sheet. Ops: Selected rows, or Every op of tool ... (the tool of the row you are on). Target: type the time you want for those ops, like 12:30 (minutes:seconds) or 1:02:00 (hours:minutes:seconds) – or a change: -10% for 10% less time, +10% for 10% more. Always type the sign: a bare 10% is refused with say which way. Options: Main cutting feed only is ticked to start (plunge, retract and back feeds stay as they are). Tick Speeds too to change speeds by the same amount. Press Enter or click Preview. Read each op: time, flips and MRR before and after, and the feed change. Enter only previews – it never writes. Click Apply to write it. Ctrl+Z takes it all back. Only the cutting part of the time can change. Rapids, tool changes and dwells stay the same, so a very short target may not be reachable. The window says how close it gets. Speeds too: the flips column then gets a * and a note. Flips are counted from cut time, so a faster speed looks like fewer flips – but a faster speed wears the edge sooner. Check the edge time with Inspection & inserts. (1) The target. (2) Main cutting feed only. (3) Each op, before and after. (4) Apply – Preview and Enter never write."},
{p:"target-time.html",a:"example",t:"Hit a target time",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"The bore ops 7 and 8 take 1:59:24 together. The cell lead wants 10% off. Select a cell in op 7's row and op 8's row. Click Hit a target time. Type -10%, press Enter. The main feeds go up 11.2%. Read the preview, then click Apply. Op feed time before time after 7 Rough Bore ID 0.01 → 0.01112 1:44:00 1:33:35 8 Finish Bore ID 0.01 → 0.01112 0:15:24 0:13:51 Both 1:59:24 1:47:26 (-10.0%) Flips per part stay at 11 for the tool. Then check the insert: is the new feed inside the maker's range for that insert and that depth? If not, the time is a wish, not a plan."},
{p:"target-time.html",a:"messages",t:"Hit a target time",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"Press Enter (or Preview) to see what this changes, op by op. Type the target, then press Enter or click Preview. '10%' - say which way: +10% for more, -10% for less. Type the sign. -10% means 10% less time. ... the target 1:47:28 is out of reach The feeds could not get exactly there (limits, or time that is not cutting). The line says how close it got. 2 cell(s) held at a limit: ... Those feeds could not go as far as the others. The other ops make up the difference where they can. Not applied yet - Apply, or Revert to follow the sheet again. A target is typed. The window keeps it while you click around the sheet to check. The sheet changed since this was worked out - here it is again. Check it, then Apply. Something changed the cells. The plan was worked out again."},
{p:"target-time.html",a:"related",t:"Hit a target time",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Slowest ops – where to aim Scale % How the cycle time estimate works Scenarios – save this idea and compare"},
{p:"check.html",a:"",t:"Program check",h:"",k:"program check review prove out before the floor findings look at FYI ignore edge runs long usual edge time CSS no max RPM coolant off air cutting tool changes regenerate no comment wrong unit Check the program before it goes to the floor A list of things worth a second look.",x:"Ribbon: Parameter Table tab › Improve › Program check"},
{p:"check.html",a:"what",t:"Program check",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"A second pair of eyes. It looks over every op and makes a Program check sheet of findings. Each finding says why it matters, how to fix it, and links to the op. Look at findings are worth a look before the program runs; FYI ones are just good to know. It changes nothing."},
{p:"check.html",a:"when",t:"Program check",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"Before a program goes to the floor for the first time. After you edited a lot of ops. When you take over a program someone else wrote."},
{p:"check.html",a:"steps",t:"Program check",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Click Program check. The Program check sheet opens. The top lines say how many findings there are: so many to look at, so many FYI. Read the Look at findings first (they are at the top, most useful first). Each line has the Op, the Tool, What was found, Why it matters and How to fix. Click the op link to go to that op's row. Fix it there (or in Mastercam). If a finding is fine as it is (you meant it), set its Ignore cell to yes. It stays ignored on the next check – and after the next dump too (it is kept in the part's .ptconfig file). Ignored findings show greyed at the bottom. Run the check again after your fixes. It makes the sheet new each time and changes nothing on your other sheets. What it looks for Finding Kind When An edge runs much longer than usual (the main check) Look at A tool's edge time (its cut time per part ÷ flips per part) is more than 1.25 × the insert's Usual edge time that you type in the inserts table. With none typed: more than 1.5 × the other tools carrying the same insert in this program (their middle value) and at least 2 minutes longer. Ops needing regeneration Look at needs_regen is yes: the toolpath, and its times, are out of date. A feed that looks like the wrong unit Look at Per minute but under 1 in (25 mm), or per rev but over 0.1 in (2.5 mm). CSS with no max RPM Look at Near the center, CSS asks for a huge RPM. Set a max RPM. Lots of air cutting Look at Over 30% of an op's cut time in air, and over 1 minute. The same tool at different speeds or feeds FYI Same tool and kind of op, speeds or feeds more than 10% apart. Extra tool changes FYI One tool put in more than once in a toolpath group. Coolant off on a cutting op FYI Usually forgotten. Dry on purpose? Ignore it. Ops with no comment FYI The setup sheet and the operator see a blank name. Two things it does not do: it never flags tool inspection being off by itself (plenty of ops need no stops), and the words in an inspection comment do not decide what it counts as a flip. For a tool that never flips, its edge time is its cut time per part × the parts per edge you typed. The Program check sheet: Look at first, then FYI. Click an op to go to it."},
{p:"check.html",a:"example",t:"Program check",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"On the Oil Spool Head the check finds 19 things: 3 to look at, 16 FYI. Two of them: Severity Op What was found How to fix Ignore Look at ops 7, 8 (T12) T12 cuts about 9:00 on each edge – T11, the other C 80° r0.031 tool in this program, runs 5:15. Turn the insert sooner on T12: insp_time about 5:15 (Inspection & inserts on the ribbon) – or, if its edge really lasts, type the usual edge time for that insert on Tools. FYI op 2 (T1) op 2 cuts with no coolant on (coolant: Off). Turn the coolant on for op 2 – or Ignore this if it runs dry on purpose. yes The PrimeTurning rough runs dry on purpose, so its coolant finding is set to yes in Ignore. On the next check it moves to the bottom, greyed. The T12 finding stays until you fix it – or type a Usual edge time of 9:00 or more for the insert, if 9:00 is normal in your shop."},
{p:"check.html",a:"messages",t:"Program check",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"Nothing found - the program looks clean. Nothing to look at (ignored findings are not counted). 19 findings ... 3 to look at, 16 FYI The count, at the top of the sheet. A finding came back after a new dump It is a different finding now (another op or another tool), so it is not the one you ignored. Set Ignore to yes again. ? (the user manual is not installed) in the top corner The link to this page could not find the manual. See Install. The program check stopped: ... Something went wrong while checking. Save your work and tell whoever looks after the tool what it said."},
{p:"check.html",a:"related",t:"Program check",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Slowest ops Inspection & inserts Change report"},
{p:"scenarios.html",a:"",t:"Scenarios",h:"",k:"scenarios what-if compare two ideas save restore delete side by side option A option B Compare two ideas Save each one, then see them side by side.",x:"Ribbon: Parameter Table tab › Improve › Scenarios"},
{p:"scenarios.html",a:"what",t:"Scenarios",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"A scenario is your set of edits, saved under a name inside the workbook. Save one idea, try another, then compare them side by side: cycle time, cutting time, flips and MRR for each, and every parameter each one changes."},
{p:"scenarios.html",a:"when",t:"Scenarios",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"“Faster feeds or faster speeds – which saves more?” You want to keep a set of edits to come back to, without loading it yet. You want to show the boss two options."},
{p:"scenarios.html",a:"steps",t:"Scenarios",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Make your first set of edits on the sheet. Scenarios › Save the changes as a scenario. Type a name, click OK. Put the sheet back (Back to the dump › Everything) and make the second set of edits. Save it under another name. Scenarios › Compare scenarios. A Scenarios sheet opens with the totals and every changed parameter, side by side. To go on with one: Scenarios › Restore a scenario. The sheet goes back to the dump, then that scenario's values go in. Edits on the sheet that were not saved are kept first, as a scenario named with the date and time, e.g. (before restore 2026-10-09 22:40) – nothing is lost. Delete a scenario removes one you no longer want (the sheet is not changed). (1) The name. (2) OK. The list shows every edit it keeps. The Scenarios sheet: totals for each idea, then every parameter they change."},
{p:"scenarios.html",a:"example",t:"Scenarios",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"Two ideas for the Oil Spool Head: faster bore feeds (ops 7 and 8 at 0.012), or a faster facing speed (op 1 at 250 SFM). Scenario Cells changed Cycle time vs the dump Flips / part As dumped 0 12:46:38 +0:00 89 Bore feed 0.012 2 12:26:50 -19:49 (-2.6%) 89 Face 250 SFM 1 12:42:26 -4:12 (-0.5%) 89 The bore feeds save about five times more. Restore Bore feed 0.012, save, and load it."},
{p:"scenarios.html",a:"messages",t:"Scenarios",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"No scenarios saved yet - edit the sheet, then Save scenario. There is nothing to restore, compare or delete yet. A scenario called (before restore 2026-10-09 22:40) Made for you when you restored another scenario: the edits that were on the sheet then. Restore it to get them back, or delete it. ... REPLACES the one saved ... A scenario by that name exists. OK writes over it. Type a new name to keep both. 'Bore feed 0.012' restored - 2 cell(s) set, 1 refused ... A value could not go back: its op or column is gone (the sheet was dumped again), or it is outside its limits."},
{p:"scenarios.html",a:"related",t:"Scenarios",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Undo and back to the dump Change report Hit a target time"},
{p:"undo.html",a:"",t:"Undo",h:"",k:"undo ctrl+z mistake take back revert back to the dump put back selected cells whole rows everything reset start over Undo a mistake in Excel Ctrl+Z, Undo, or back to the dump.",x:"Ribbon: Parameter Table tab › Undo and review › Undo / Back to the dump"},
{p:"undo.html",a:"what",t:"Undo",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"Two ways back in Excel: Undo (or Ctrl+Z) takes back the last Parameter Table command – a whole Set, Scale, Copy or Apply at once. Press it again to go further back, up to 20 commands. Back to the dump puts cells back to the values they had when the sheet was dumped: the Selected cells, the Selected ops (whole rows), or Everything. To take back a load that already went into Mastercam, see Undo a load."},
{p:"undo.html",a:"when",t:"Undo",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"Undo: right after a command did something you did not mean. Back to the dump: when an idea did not work out and you want a clean start on some ops – or on all of them."},
{p:"undo.html",a:"steps",t:"Undo",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Undo a command Press Ctrl+Z, or click Undo on the ribbon. The status bar says what was undone and how many cells went back. Press again to undo the command before that. A cell you have typed over since the command is left alone – Undo never throws away your typing. The status bar counts those cells as “left alone (changed since)”. Plain typing in a cell is undone by Excel's own Ctrl+Z, as usual. Back to the dump Select the cells (or any cell in the op rows) to put back. Click Back to the dump and pick Selected cells, Selected ops (whole rows) or Everything. Everything asks first and says how many cells. Save a scenario first if you might want these edits again. The cells go back to their dumped values; the yellow goes away."},
{p:"undo.html",a:"example",t:"Undo",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"You used Set to put 0.012 in the feed of ops 1, 7 and 8. Then you typed 0.011 into op 8's feed by hand. Now you click Undo on the ribbon: Op Dumped After Set Now After Undo 1 feed 0.01 0.012 0.012 0.01 7 feed 0.01 0.012 0.012 0.01 8 feed 0.01 0.012 0.011 (typed) 0.011 (left alone) Ops 1 and 7 go back. Op 8 keeps your 0.011, because you typed it after the Set. To clear op 8 too, use Back to the dump › Selected cells on it. The ribbon's Undo button always takes back the last command. Ctrl+Z does the same right after a command; right after you typed in a cell, it takes back that typing first (Excel's own undo)."},
{p:"undo.html",a:"messages",t:"Undo",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"undid Set selected - 3 cell(s) put back. Done. ... 1 left alone (changed since). That cell was typed over after the command. Your typing stays. nothing to undo. No command to take back (or more than 20 ago, or the workbook was closed since). nothing to revert - the sheet is as dumped. Everything found no edits. 3 cell(s) put back to their dumped value. Back to the dump is done."},
{p:"undo.html",a:"related",t:"Undo",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Undo a load (in Mastercam) List changes Scenarios"},
{p:"changes.html",a:"",t:"List changes",h:"",k:"list changes what did I change edits old new dumped now review before load Changes sheet See what I changed Every changed cell, old and new.",x:"Ribbon: Parameter Table tab › Undo and review › List changes"},
{p:"changes.html",a:"what",t:"List changes",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"Makes a Changes sheet: one line for every cell that is different from the dump – the op, its type and comment, the parameter, the dumped value and the value now. Click a parameter to jump to its cell."},
{p:"changes.html",a:"when",t:"List changes",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"Before you load: one last look at everything that will go into the part. When you have lost track of what you changed. Without macros, the changes column on the main sheet counts each row's edits, and Changed ops shows only those rows."},
{p:"changes.html",a:"steps",t:"List changes",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Click List changes. The Changes sheet opens. Read down the list. Each line is one cell: dumped is the old value, now the new. Click a blue parameter name to go to that cell on the main sheet. Run it again any time – it makes a fresh list each time. The Changes sheet: click a parameter to jump to it."},
{p:"changes.html",a:"example",t:"List changes",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"After raising the bore feeds (ops 7 and 8), the Changes sheet reads: op_idn type comment parameter dumped now 7 ROUGH Rough Bore ID feed 0.01 0.012 8 FINISH Finish Bore ID feed 0.01 0.012 Worked-out columns (times, flips, MRR) are not listed – only what you changed."},
{p:"changes.html",a:"messages",t:"List changes",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"No changes from the dump. The sheet is as dumped (or every edit was put back)."},
{p:"changes.html",a:"related",t:"List changes",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Change report – the printable version, with the savings Load the changes Back to the dump"},
{p:"report.html",a:"",t:"Change report",h:"",k:"change report print one page before after savings cycle time per batch insert cost flips why reason boss record paperwork PDF Print a report of my changes One page: before, after, and why.",x:"Ribbon: Parameter Table tab › Undo and review › Change report"},
{p:"report.html",a:"what",t:"Change report",h:"What this is for",k:"",x:"A one-page report of what you changed and what it saves: cycle time per part and per batch, insert flips, and insert cost – before and after. Every change is listed, with a Why column for you to type the reason. Print it or save it as a PDF for the job folder, your lead, or the next programmer."},
{p:"report.html",a:"when",t:"Change report",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"Before you load: to get a change signed off. After you load: to keep a record of what changed and why."},
{p:"report.html",a:"steps",t:"Change report",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Type the batch quantity on the Summary and the insert costs on the Tools page first – the report uses them. Click Change report. The Change report sheet opens, with the part's name and the date it was dumped at the top. Read Before -> after: cycle time and cutting time per part (and per batch), insert flips, inserts used and insert cost per part, and insert cost per batch – as dumped, now, and the change. Read Every change, by op: each op with its time change, then each parameter, as dumped and now. Type a short reason in the Why (type the reason) column (“insert box says 0.008-0.016”). Your reasons are kept when you make the report again. Print it: File › Print (or Ctrl+P). It is set up landscape, one page wide. Pick Microsoft Print to PDF to make a PDF. The Change report: totals first, then every change by op, with room for the reason."},
{p:"report.html",a:"example",t:"Change report",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"Oil Spool Head, bore feeds raised to 0.012, batch of 20 parts (typed on the Summary): Before After Saved Cycle time per part 12:46:38 12:26:50 0:19:49 Cycle time per batch (20) about 255:33 about 248:57 about 6:36 Insert flips per part 89 89 0 Op Parameter As dumped Now Op time change Why (type the reason) op 7 – T12 ROUGH – Rough Bore ID Feed 0.01 0.012 1:44:00 → 1:26:45 (-17:15) Insert box says 0.008-0.016 op 8 – T12 FINISH – Finish Bore ID Feed 0.01 0.012 15:24 → 12:50 (-2:34) Same tool; finish checked on first part"},
{p:"report.html",a:"messages",t:"Change report",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"No changes from the dump yet - edit 'Lathe params' first. Nothing to report: the sheet is as dumped. Type the cost per insert on the Tools page for the insert cost per part and per batch. The cost lines are blank until you type the insert costs. The change report stopped: ... Something went wrong making it. Save your work and tell whoever looks after the tool what it said."},
{p:"report.html",a:"related",t:"Change report",h:"Related",k:"",x:"List changes Scenarios Summary page"},
{p:"estimate.html",a:"what",t:"The cycle time estimate",h:"What this is for",k:"cycle time estimate est_cycle_time time_change live how accurate feed speed CSS max RPM per rev per min rapids tool change dwell regenerate depth",x:"Each op row has cycle_time (Mastercam's own time, as dumped) and est_cycle_time (the live estimate). They start the same. When you change the feed, speed, CSS/RPM, max RPM or per rev / per min on a row, the estimate moves at once – and so do the Summary's totals. This page says how it works and where it can be wrong."},
{p:"estimate.html",a:"when",t:"The cycle time estimate",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"Whenever you judge a change by its time – and before you trust a big saving."},
{p:"estimate.html",a:"steps",t:"The cycle time estimate",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"How the estimate is worked out: At the dump, the tool walks each op's toolpath, move by move: how long each cutting move is, and at what diameter. For each move it works out the time: per minute: length ÷ feed. Per rev: length ÷ (feed × RPM). With CSS, the RPM at that diameter is SFM × 3.82 ÷ diameter – but never more than the op's max RPM. These sums are written into the sheet as formulas tied to the row's own cells. Change a cell and the formula follows. Everything else – rapids, tool changes, dwells – is kept as it was. It does not change with feed or speed. What it cannot know until you regenerate: a new depth of cut, stepover, stock to leave or number of passes changes the toolpath itself. The estimate does not move for those. Load, regenerate, and dump again to see their time. On real parts, the estimate matches Mastercam's own cycle time to within seconds per op."},
{p:"estimate.html",a:"example",t:"The cycle time estimate",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"Op 7, Rough Bore ID: 1:44:00 in all, of which 1:43:28 is cutting. The feed goes from 0.010 to 0.012 per rev. Before After feed 0.010 0.012 Cutting time 1:43:28 1:26:13 (× 0.010 ÷ 0.012) Everything else 0:00:32 0:00:32 est_cycle_time 1:44:00 1:26:45 time_change +0:00:00 -0:17:15 Where max RPM bites. Facing with CSS at 200 SFM and a max of 3500 RPM: at 1″ diameter the spindle turns 764 RPM; near the center, at 0.2″, it would need 3820 RPM but stops at 3500. Raising the SFM speeds up the outside of the face, not the middle. The estimate knows this, move by move. (1) The new feed. (2) Mastercam's time, as dumped. (3) The live estimate."},
{p:"estimate.html",a:"messages",t:"The cycle time estimate",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"est_cycle_time is blank The op had no toolpath to walk when dumped (it needed regenerating), or it is a kind with no moves (manual entry). needs_regen says yes The toolpath is out of date, so its times are too. Regenerate and dump again. The estimate did not move when I changed a depth Right – see the box above. Depths change the toolpath; regenerate to see the new time."},
{p:"estimate.html",a:"related",t:"The cycle time estimate",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Summary page Hit a target time Speed & feed Estimate in the word list"},
{p:"flips.html",a:"what",t:"How insert flips are counted",h:"What this is for",k:"insert flips how counted tool inspection stop comment ROTATE FLIP CHANGE INDEX edge clock edge life insp_time flips_part flips_est flips_uncommented edge_limit inserts per part",x:"An insert flip is a fresh cutting edge: the operator rotates, flips or changes the insert. Flips per part tell you how many inserts a part uses – and what they cost. This page explains where the numbers in the flip columns come from, so you can trust them (or know when not to)."},
{p:"flips.html",a:"when",t:"How insert flips are counted",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"When the flips on the sheet look too high or too low, or before you change insp_time or an inspection comment."},
{p:"flips.html",a:"steps",t:"How insert flips are counted",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"The tool counts flips in three steps, for every op: Stops with a comment. Mastercam's Tool inspection settings stop the machine (every so much cutting time, distance, or number of cuts). Each stop has a comment. If the comment says ROTATE, FLIP, CHANGE or INDEX, the stop is a flip. Other stops (CHECK INSERT) are just a look. These are read from the toolpath as it is – column flips – and follow your edits live in flips_est. Stops with no comment (the op's inspection comment switch, insp_comment_on, is off). They leave nothing in the toolpath, so the tool works them out with an edge clock: it adds up the tool's cutting time, and every time the edge has cut its edge_limit, that is one flip. edge_limit is the op's insp_time, or, if the op has none, the tool's Edge life on the Tools page (8:00 to start). The clock carries over from one op of the tool to the next. Column flips_uncommented. Add them up. flips_part = flips_est + flips_uncommented, in whole flips. The Tools page adds each tool's ops (counting the copies a transform makes); the inserts table divides by the edges per insert. Why the two counts can differ: the edge keeps wearing from op to op, but Mastercam's timer starts again."},
{p:"flips.html",a:"example",t:"How insert flips are counted",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"Tool T5 runs two ops: A cuts 5:00, B cuts 7:00. Its ops stop for inspection with no comment; the tool's edge life is 8:00. op A op B Tool T5 Cutting time 5:00 7:00 12:00 edge_after (edge clock leaving the op) 5:00 4:00 flips_uncommented 0 1 1 flip per part Now say op B's feed goes up 40%. Its cutting time drops to 5:00, the clock reaches only 10:00 – and still one flip. Up 70%: 4:07, the clock ends at 9:07, still one flip. The flips only drop when the whole tool cuts less than one edge life. If the stops had a comment like ROTATE INSERT, Mastercam's own timer would decide: it starts again in op B, neither op reaches 8:00, so there would be 0 flips. That is why the comment you pick matters."},
{p:"flips.html",a:"messages",t:"How insert flips are counted",h:"What the messages mean",k:"",x:"flips_why says time 8 + end 1 8 flips came from the time setting and 1 from the stop at the end of the op. flip_longest is much longer than insp_time The op stops only between cuts, so a long pass runs past the setting before it can stop. See insp_between_cuts in the word list."},
{p:"flips.html",a:"related",t:"How insert flips are counted",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Inspection & inserts – use every edge evenly Tools page Edge life, insp_time"},
{p:"words.html",a:"what",t:"Word list",h:"What this is for",k:"word list glossary meaning what does it mean column names definitions terms abbreviations Understand a column or a word Every column and term in plain words.",x:"Plain meanings for the words in this manual and on the sheet. The first part is general words. The second part is every column, by the name it has on the sheet (row 2 of Lathe params)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"when",t:"Word list",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"When a word or a column name is new to you. On the sheet itself, hover over a column name for the same help."},
{p:"words.html",a:"steps",t:"Word list",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Press Ctrl+F in your browser and type the word or the column name, e.g. max_ss. Or click a letter below to jump to it. Follow the See also links for the bigger picture. A B C D E F G I L M N O P R S T U W Y Columns"},
{p:"words.html",a:"example",t:"Word list",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"Row 2 of the sheet says feed_mode and the cell says per rev. Look up feed_mode in the columns below: per rev (IPR / mm per rev) or per min (IPM / mm per min) for feed. Then per rev / per min above it tells you what that means at the machine. On the sheet In plain words feed 0.012, feed_mode per rev The tool moves 0.012″ for every turn of the spindle (G95 F.012). speed 200, speed_mode CSS 200 feet per minute at the cutting edge; the RPM changes with the diameter (G96 S200). max_ss 3500 The spindle never goes above 3500 RPM, however small the diameter (G50 S3500)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"related",t:"Word list",h:"Related",k:"",x:"The main sheet Tools page How insert flips are counted The cycle time estimate"},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-add-in",t:"Word list",h:"Add-in",k:"Add-in Add-in",x:"A program that plugs into Mastercam and adds commands to it. This tool is one."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-air-cutting",t:"Word list",h:"Air cutting (air_pct)",k:"Air cutting (air pct) Air cutting (air_pct)",x:"Time the tool spends feeding but touching nothing – usually a pass that starts too far out, or stock that is not there any more. Cutting air costs time and nothing else."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-batch",t:"Word list",h:"Batch",k:"Batch Batch",x:"How many parts you run in one go. Type it on the Summary to get time and insert cost per batch."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-blue",t:"Word list",h:"Blue cell",k:"Blue cell Blue cell",x:"A worked-out number (time, flips, MRR) that moved because of your edits. You did not type it."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-cell",t:"Word list",h:"Cell, row, column",k:"Cell, row, column Cell, row, column",x:"A cell is one box of the spreadsheet. A row goes across (one op); a column goes down (one setting). Columns have letters at the top (A, B, C); rows have numbers on the left."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-chip-thinning",t:"Word list",h:"Chip thinning, chip thickness",k:"Chip thinning, chip thickness Chip thinning, chip thickness",x:"With a round insert, or an insert with a lead angle, the chip is thinner than the feed: real chip = feed × sin(entering angle) . A chip that is too thin rubs instead of cutting and wears the insert. To get the chip you want, program a higher feed. See Speed & feed ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-column-group",t:"Word list",h:"Column group",k:"Column group Column group",x:"A set of columns under one name in row 1 (Feeds and speeds, Coolant ...). Groups fold away with the small + / - above the columns. See The main sheet ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-coolant",t:"Word list",h:"Coolant: Off, Flood, Mist, Thru-tool",k:"Coolant: Off, Flood, Mist, Thru-tool Coolant: Off, Flood, Mist, Thru-tool",x:"Flood: a stream over the cut. Mist: a fine spray. Thru-tool: through the tool or the holder, at the edge. Machines are set up one of two ways: one coolant setting per op ( coolant , also called V9 style), or coolants turned on before , with or after the move, by the machine's own names ( coolant_before , coolant_with , coolant_after , also called X style). See Coolant ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-css",t:"Word list",h:"CSS (constant surface speed)",k:"CSS (constant surface speed) CSS (constant surface speed)",x:"The spindle speeds up as the tool moves toward the center, so the edge always sees the same surface speed . G96 on most controls. The opposite is fixed RPM (G97). Always set a max RPM with CSS."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-ctrl-z",t:"Word list",h:"Ctrl+Z",k:"Ctrl+Z Ctrl+Z",x:"Undo. Hold Ctrl and press Z . See Undo ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-cut-time",t:"Word list",h:"Cutting time (cut_seconds_est)",k:"Cutting time (cut seconds est) Cutting time (cut_seconds_est)",x:"The part of the cycle time spent feeding (cutting or air cutting). Rapids and tool changes are not in it."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-cycle-time",t:"Word list",h:"Cycle time",k:"Cycle time Cycle time",x:"How long the op (or the whole part) takes. cycle_time is Mastercam's own figure, as dumped."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-depth-of-cut",t:"Word list",h:"Depth of cut (step, DOC)",k:"Depth of cut (step, DOC) Depth of cut (step, DOC)",x:"How deep each roughing pass cuts. On a lathe, usually per side (radius). A finish op's step is the stepover between finish passes."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-dropdown",t:"Word list",h:"Dropdown",k:"Dropdown Dropdown",x:"A cell with a small arrow on its right when you click it. Click the arrow and pick from the list."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-dump",t:"Word list",h:"Dump",k:"Dump Dump",x:"Writing the part's ops out to an Excel workbook. See Dump to Excel ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-dynamic",t:"Word list",h:"Dynamic (dynamic rough, dynamic mill)",k:"Dynamic (dynamic rough, dynamic mill) Dynamic (dynamic rough, dynamic mill)",x:"Toolpaths that keep the tool's engagement even, with a small stepover and a high feed. Their feed and stepover go together – see chip thinning ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-edge",t:"Word list",h:"Edge",k:"Edge Edge",x:"One cutting corner of an insert. A CNMG has 4 usable edges (2 on each side); a VCMT has 2; a round insert has many."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-edge-life",t:"Word list",h:"Edge life",k:"Edge life Edge life",x:"How much cutting time one edge lasts before it should be turned. The Tools page's Edge life (fallback) is used for ops that stop for inspection but set no insp_time of their own. Typed as minutes:seconds ( 8:00 )."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-entering-angle",t:"Word list",h:"Entering angle, lead angle",k:"Entering angle, lead angle Entering angle, lead angle",x:"The angle between the cutting edge and the direction of feed. A 90° entering angle (0° lead) makes a chip as thick as the feed. A 45° entering angle makes it about 0.7 of the feed. Makers give one or the other – entering angle = 90° − lead angle."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-estimate",t:"Word list",h:"Estimate (est_cycle_time)",k:"Estimate (est cycle time) Estimate (est_cycle_time)",x:"The live cycle time: it starts equal to Mastercam's and moves with your feed and speed edits. See The cycle time estimate ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-feed",t:"Word list",h:"Feed",k:"Feed Feed",x:"How fast the tool moves while cutting: per rev or per minute . On the sheet, the number is feed (always positive) and the unit is feed_mode ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-flip",t:"Word list",h:"Flip (insert flip)",k:"Flip (insert flip) Flip (insert flip)",x:"Turning, flipping or changing the insert to a fresh edge. The tool counts a tool inspection stop as a flip when its comment says ROTATE, FLIP, CHANGE or INDEX – and works out stops with no comment from the edge life. See How insert flips are counted ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-grey",t:"Word list",h:"Grey cell",k:"Grey cell Grey cell",x:"A cell you cannot type in: read-only, or a setting that is not part of that kind of op."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-insert",t:"Word list",h:"Insert",k:"Insert Insert",x:"The replaceable carbide (or other) cutting tip clamped in the holder. The Tools page names each one by shape and nose radius, like C 80° r0.031 (an 80° diamond, 0.031″ nose radius)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-insert-size",t:"Word list",h:"Insert size (IC)",k:"Insert size (IC) Insert size (IC)",x:"The size of an insert's inscribed circle – for a round insert, its diameter. Chip thinning on round inserts needs it."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-inspection-comment",t:"Word list",h:"Inspection comment (insp_comment)",k:"Inspection comment (insp comment) Inspection comment (insp_comment)",x:"The message that comes out at a tool inspection stop, for the operator: ROTATE INSERT , CHECK INSERT ... Its words decide whether the stop counts as a flip."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-insp-time",t:"Word list",h:"Tool inspection, insp_time",k:"Tool inspection, insp time Tool inspection, insp_time",x:"Mastercam can stop the machine during an op so the operator can look at or turn the insert: every so much cutting time ( insp_time , minutes:seconds), distance, or number of cuts, and at the end. These settings are the Tool inspection column group."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-load",t:"Word list",h:"Load",k:"Load Load",x:"Putting the edited values from the workbook back into the part. See Load the changes ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-macro",t:"Word list",h:"Macro",k:"Macro Macro",x:"A small program inside the workbook. With macros (an .xlsm file) you get the Parameter Table tab on Excel's ribbon. Without them ( .xlsx ) the workbook still works and still loads."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-manual-entry",t:"Word list",h:"Manual entry",k:"Manual entry Manual entry",x:"An op that puts your own text into the NC program, as a comment or as code. Its text is manual_text ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-max-rpm",t:"Word list",h:"Max RPM (max_ss)",k:"Max RPM (max ss) Max RPM (max_ss)",x:"The highest spindle speed the op may use. With CSS , the RPM climbs as the diameter shrinks; max RPM stops it there (G50 S on many lathes). Too high near the center is a safety risk; too low slows small diameters."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-median",t:"Word list",h:"Median",k:"Median Median",x:"The middle value: put the values in order and take the one in the middle (with an even count, halfway between the two middle ones). Unlike the average, one odd part does not pull it far. The shop workbook gives the median speed, feed, depth and edge time of each insert."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-mixed",t:"Word list",h:"Mixed",k:"Mixed Mixed",x:"In a window that works on several ops: the ops do not all have the same value. Whatever you apply replaces them all."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-mrr",t:"Word list",h:"MRR, material removed",k:"MRR, material removed MRR, material removed",x:"Metal removal rate: how much material is cut per minute (in³/min or cm³/min). mrr is the theory while cutting (12 × SFM × feed per rev × depth for turning). mrr_avg is the real one over the whole op, including air and rapids. removed is the total the op takes off – fixed by the toolpath."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-nci",t:"Word list",h:"NCI",k:"NCI NCI",x:"Mastercam's own record of a toolpath's moves, before the post turns it into G-code. The tool reads it for times, lengths and stops."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-op",t:"Word list",h:"Op (operation)",k:"Op (operation) Op (operation)",x:"One toolpath in Mastercam's Operation Manager: a rough, a finish, a groove, a drill ... Each op is one row of the sheet."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-op-number",t:"Word list",h:"Op number (op_idn)",k:"Op number (op idn) Op number (op_idn)",x:"The op's ID number in Mastercam. It is how the load finds the right op – never change it."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-operation-manager",t:"Word list",h:"Operation Manager",k:"Operation Manager Operation Manager",x:"The list of ops in Mastercam (the Toolpaths tab). The sheet keeps its order."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-per-rev",t:"Word list",h:"Per rev / per min (IPR, IPM)",k:"Per rev / per min (IPR, IPM) Per rev / per min (IPR, IPM)",x:"Per rev : how far the tool moves for each turn of the spindle (inches per rev, IPR, or mm per rev; G95 / G99). Per min : how far it moves in a minute (IPM or mm/min; G94 / G98). Per min = per rev × RPM. Lathes mostly use per rev; live tools use per min."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-percent",t:"Word list",h:"Percent: +10%, -10%",k:"Percent: +10%, -10% Percent: +10%, -10%",x:"+10% means 10% more than now: 0.010 becomes 0.011. -10% means 10% less: 0.010 becomes 0.009. Always type the sign – a bare 10% is refused, because it does not say which way. In Hit a target time , -10% means 10% less time ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-post",t:"Word list",h:"Post",k:"Post Post",x:"The post processor: it turns Mastercam's toolpaths into G-code for your machine."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-primeturning",t:"Word list",h:"PrimeTurning",k:"PrimeTurning PrimeTurning",x:"Sandvik's turning method that cuts in both directions with a small entering angle and high feeds, using A-type and B-type inserts. Its ops keep their own feeds and speeds ( pt_rough_speed , pt_rough_feed_axial ...) – the plain feed and speed columns do not drive them."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-ptconfig",t:"Word list",h:".ptconfig file",k:".ptconfig file .ptconfig file",x:"A small file next to the part ( <part>.ptconfig ) that keeps what you typed on the Tools page and the Summary: insert names, edges, cost, parts per edge, edge life, batch quantity. The next dump starts from it. Delete it to start over."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-radius-diameter",t:"Word list",h:"Radius and diameter",k:"Radius and diameter Radius and diameter",x:"The X travel columns ( travel_x_min , travel_x_max ) are a radius – double them for the diameter. The tool works speeds at the diameter."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-regenerate",t:"Word list",h:"Regenerate",k:"Regenerate Regenerate",x:"Making Mastercam work out an op's toolpath again after its settings changed. After a load, the changed ops are marked dirty (needing regeneration). Until you regenerate, the NC code and the times are the old ones."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-ribbon",t:"Word list",h:"Ribbon",k:"Ribbon Ribbon",x:"The band of buttons across the top of Excel (or Mastercam), in tabs. The tool adds a Parameter Table tab to Excel's."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-rpm",t:"Word list",h:"RPM",k:"RPM RPM",x:"Revolutions per minute: how fast the spindle turns. With CSS the RPM changes with the diameter; with fixed RPM (G97) it does not."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-scenario",t:"Word list",h:"Scenario",k:"Scenario Scenario",x:"A set of edits saved under a name inside the workbook, to restore or compare later. See Scenarios ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-sfm",t:"Word list",h:"SFM (surface speed, m/min)",k:"SFM (surface speed, m/min) SFM (surface speed, m/min)",x:"Surface feet per minute: how fast the material passes the cutting edge. RPM = SFM × 3.82 ÷ diameter in inches. Metric: m/min, and RPM = m/min × 318.3 ÷ diameter in mm. Insert boxes give a range of SFM for each material."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-shop-workbook",t:"Word list",h:"Shop workbook",k:"Shop workbook Shop workbook",x:"The one workbook Dump a folder of parts writes after it dumps every part: Shop_compare_ and the date. It puts the parts side by side - per part, per insert and per op - so you can see how your shop runs each insert."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-status-bar",t:"Word list",h:"Status bar",k:"Status bar Status bar",x:"The thin bar along the very bottom of the Excel window. The tool writes short messages there (“3 cell(s) set ...”)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-stepover",t:"Word list",h:"Stepover",k:"Stepover Stepover",x:"How far the tool moves over between passes, side to side. For dynamic toolpaths, often given as a percent of the tool ( stepover_percent )."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-stock-to-leave",t:"Word list",h:"Stock to leave",k:"Stock to leave Stock to leave",x:"Material a rough leaves on for the finish pass, in X ( stock_x , usually per side) and Z ( stock_z )."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-tool-number",t:"Word list",h:"Tool number (T12)",k:"Tool number (T12) Tool number (T12)",x:"The turret or pocket number of the tool. In Find op , type T12 to find tool 12's ops."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-toolpath",t:"Word list",h:"Toolpath",k:"Toolpath Toolpath",x:"The moves an op makes. Mastercam works it out from the op's settings (see regenerate )."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-tooltip",t:"Word list",h:"Tooltip",k:"Tooltip Tooltip",x:"The little note that appears when you hover the mouse over something, or click a cell. Every column name and cell on the sheet has one."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-transform",t:"Word list",h:"Transform",k:"Transform Transform",x:"An op that copies other ops' toolpaths (mirror, rotate, translate). The sheet shows transforms read-only; their copies count in the part's time and flips."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-usual-edge-time",t:"Word list",h:"Usual edge time",k:"Usual edge time Usual edge time",x:"What one edge of an insert normally lasts in your shop. You type it in the last column of the Tools page's inserts table ; the Program check warns about a tool whose edge runs more than 25% longer."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-workbook",t:"Word list",h:"Workbook, sheet",k:"Workbook, sheet Workbook, sheet",x:"A workbook is one Excel file. A sheet is one page of it, chosen by the tabs along the bottom (Summary, Lathe params, Tools)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"w-yellow",t:"Word list",h:"Yellow cell",k:"Yellow cell Yellow cell",x:"A cell you changed: it differs from the dump. It goes white again if you put the dumped value back."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-changes",t:"Word list",h:"changes",k:"changes changes",x:"How many cells in this row differ from the dump. Edited cells are highlighted."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-comment",t:"Word list",h:"comment",k:"comment comment",x:"The operation's comment, as the Operation Manager shows it. Edit it here and it loads back (up to 119 characters)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-group_name",t:"Word list",h:"group_name",k:"group name group_name",x:"The toolpath group the operation is in."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-needs_regen",t:"Word list",h:"needs_regen",k:"needs regen needs_regen",x:"yes = the toolpath is out of date; regenerate before posting. Stats are stale until then. See also: regenerate ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-op_idn",t:"Word list",h:"op_idn",k:"op idn op_idn",x:"The operation's ID - how a load finds its row's operation. Do not change."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-tool",t:"Word list",h:"tool",k:"tool tool",x:"Tool number."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-tool_name",t:"Word list",h:"tool_name",k:"tool name tool_name",x:"The tool's description."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-tool_radius",t:"Word list",h:"tool_radius",k:"tool radius tool_radius",x:"The tool radius the percent columns are a percent of (dynamic rough)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-type",t:"Word list",h:"type",k:"type type",x:"The kind of operation - ROUGH, FINISH, FACE, GROOVE, DRILL, MANUAL, DYNAMIC MILL and so on."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-units",t:"Word list",h:"units",k:"units units",x:"in or mm - the units every length on this row is in."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-back_feed",t:"Word list",h:"back_feed",k:"back feed back_feed",x:"Dynamic mill back feed - the feed of the repositioning moves (per minute)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-cut_length",t:"Word list",h:"cut_length",k:"cut length cut_length",x:"Total length of the feed moves (lines and arcs) in the toolpath."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-cycle_time",t:"Word list",h:"cycle_time",k:"cycle time cycle_time",x:"Mastercam's estimated cycle time for this operation, h:mm:ss (matches the Operation Manager to within seconds)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-cycle_time_raw",t:"Word list",h:"cycle_time_raw",k:"cycle time raw cycle_time_raw",x:"The cycle time in seconds, as Mastercam returned it."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-dcut_fin_n",t:"Word list",h:"dcut_fin_n",k:"dcut fin n dcut_fin_n",x:"Mill depth cuts: number of finish cuts."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-dcut_finish",t:"Word list",h:"dcut_finish",k:"dcut finish dcut_finish",x:"Mill depth cuts: finish step."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-dcut_rough",t:"Word list",h:"dcut_rough",k:"dcut rough dcut_rough",x:"Mill depth cuts: max rough step."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-est_cycle_time",t:"Word list",h:"est_cycle_time",k:"est cycle time est_cycle_time",x:"LIVE estimate: Mastercam's cycle time, moved by your feed / speed / CSS / max_ss edits on this row. Equals cycle_time until you edit. See also: estimate ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-est_seconds",t:"Word list",h:"est_seconds",k:"est seconds est_seconds",x:"The live estimate in seconds (the formula behind est_cycle_time)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-feed_groups",t:"Word list",h:"feed_groups",k:"feed groups feed_groups",x:"Feed-move length at each feed rate, longest first - a dynamic mill's back feed is its own entry."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-fr_override",t:"Word list",h:"fr_override",k:"fr override fr_override",x:"Contour feed rate override, used when fr_override_on is 1."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-mcut_fin_n",t:"Word list",h:"mcut_fin_n",k:"mcut fin n mcut_fin_n",x:"Mill multi passes: number of finish passes."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-mcut_rough_n",t:"Word list",h:"mcut_rough_n",k:"mcut rough n mcut_rough_n",x:"Mill multi passes: number of rough passes."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-rapid_length",t:"Word list",h:"rapid_length",k:"rapid length rapid_length",x:"Total length of the rapid moves WITHIN the operation - backplot also counts the moves to and from it, so its figure is larger."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-ss_override",t:"Word list",h:"ss_override",k:"ss override ss_override",x:"Contour spindle speed override, used when ss_override_on is 1."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-time_change",t:"Word list",h:"time_change",k:"time change time_change",x:"How much the live estimate differs from Mastercam's cycle time."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-travel_x_max",t:"Word list",h:"travel_x_max",k:"travel x max travel_x_max",x:"Largest X the toolpath reaches - a RADIUS (double it for diameter)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-travel_x_min",t:"Word list",h:"travel_x_min",k:"travel x min travel_x_min",x:"Smallest X the toolpath reaches - a RADIUS (double it for diameter)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-travel_z_max",t:"Word list",h:"travel_z_max",k:"travel z max travel_z_max",x:"Largest Z the toolpath reaches, in the tool plane."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-travel_z_min",t:"Word list",h:"travel_z_min",k:"travel z min travel_z_min",x:"Smallest Z the toolpath reaches, in the tool plane."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-feed",t:"Word list",h:"feed",k:"feed feed",x:"Cutting feed rate - always positive here. Per rev or per minute is set in feed_mode. See also: per rev ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-feed_mode",t:"Word list",h:"feed_mode",k:"feed mode feed_mode",x:"per rev (IPR / mm per rev) or per min (IPM / mm per min) for feed. See also: per rev ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-max_ss",t:"Word list",h:"max_ss",k:"max ss max_ss",x:"Maximum spindle speed (RPM) - the cap when running CSS. See also: max rpm ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-plunge",t:"Word list",h:"plunge",k:"plunge plunge",x:"Plunge feed rate - per rev or per minute is set in plunge_mode."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-plunge_mode",t:"Word list",h:"plunge_mode",k:"plunge mode plunge_mode",x:"per rev or per min for plunge. See also: per rev ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-plunge_surf_fin",t:"Word list",h:"plunge_surf_fin",k:"plunge surf fin plunge_surf_fin",x:"1 = plunge holds a surface finish value instead of a feed rate."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-retract",t:"Word list",h:"retract",k:"retract retract",x:"Retract feed rate - per rev or per minute is set in retract_mode."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-retract_mode",t:"Word list",h:"retract_mode",k:"retract mode retract_mode",x:"per rev or per min for retract. See also: per rev ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-speed",t:"Word list",h:"speed",k:"speed speed",x:"Spindle speed - surface speed when speed_mode is CSS, RPM when it is RPM. See also: sfm ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-speed_mode",t:"Word list",h:"speed_mode",k:"speed mode speed_mode",x:"CSS = constant surface speed (speed is SFM or m/min); RPM = fixed spindle speed. See also: css ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-spindle_dir",t:"Word list",h:"spindle_dir",k:"spindle dir spindle_dir",x:"CW (M03) or CCW (M04)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-surf_fin_feed",t:"Word list",h:"surf_fin_feed",k:"surf fin feed surf_fin_feed",x:"1 = feed holds a surface finish value instead of a feed rate."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-min_step",t:"Word list",h:"min_step",k:"min step min_step",x:"Smallest depth of cut the rough will take."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-n_cuts",t:"Word list",h:"n_cuts",k:"n cuts n_cuts",x:"Number of finish passes."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-peck1",t:"Word list",h:"peck1",k:"peck1 peck1",x:"Drill: first peck depth."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-peck2",t:"Word list",h:"peck2",k:"peck2 peck2",x:"Drill: subsequent peck depth."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-step",t:"Word list",h:"step",k:"step step",x:"Rough: depth of cut per pass. Finish: stepover between finish passes. See also: depth of cut ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-stepover",t:"Word list",h:"stepover",k:"stepover stepover",x:"Dynamic rough stepover. Change this and stepover_percent follows. See also: stepover ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-stepover_percent",t:"Word list",h:"stepover_percent",k:"stepover percent stepover_percent",x:"Stepover as a percent of tool radius - calculated from stepover. Typing here replaces the formula."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-fin_stock_x",t:"Word list",h:"fin_stock_x",k:"fin stock x fin_stock_x",x:"Semi-finish passes: stock to leave in X."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-fin_stock_z",t:"Word list",h:"fin_stock_z",k:"fin stock z fin_stock_z",x:"Semi-finish passes: stock to leave in Z."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-stock_x",t:"Word list",h:"stock_x",k:"stock x stock_x",x:"Stock to leave in X. See also: stock to leave ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-stock_z",t:"Word list",h:"stock_z",k:"stock z stock_z",x:"Stock to leave in Z. See also: stock to leave ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-canned_text_raw",t:"Word list",h:"canned_text_raw",k:"canned text raw canned_text_raw",x:"The operation's canned text and coolant codes, raw."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-coolant",t:"Word list",h:"coolant",k:"coolant coolant",x:"V9 coolant - the one coolant setting on a machine set up for V9 coolant: Off, Flood, Mist or Thru-tool. See also: coolant ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-coolant_after",t:"Word list",h:"coolant_after",k:"coolant after coolant_after",x:"Coolant turned on AFTER the move - pick from this machine's coolants, or none."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-coolant_before",t:"Word list",h:"coolant_before",k:"coolant before coolant_before",x:"Coolant turned on BEFORE the move - pick from this machine's coolants, or none. See also: coolant ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-coolant_text",t:"Word list",h:"coolant_text",k:"coolant text coolant_text",x:"The V9 coolant field in words."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-coolant_with",t:"Word list",h:"coolant_with",k:"coolant with coolant_with",x:"Coolant turned on WITH the move - pick from this machine's coolants, or none."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-finish_feed",t:"Word list",h:"finish_feed",k:"finish feed finish_feed",x:"Finish-pass feed rate, used when use_finish_feed is 1."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-finish_ss",t:"Word list",h:"finish_ss",k:"finish ss finish_ss",x:"Finish-pass spindle speed, used when use_finish_ss is 1."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-finish_step",t:"Word list",h:"finish_step",k:"finish step finish_step",x:"Finish pass cut amount."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-pt_fin_feed_axial",t:"Word list",h:"pt_fin_feed_axial",k:"pt fin feed axial pt_fin_feed_axial",x:"Prime turning FINISH feed along Z; unit in pt_fin_axial_type."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-pt_fin_feed_radial",t:"Word list",h:"pt_fin_feed_radial",k:"pt fin feed radial pt_fin_feed_radial",x:"Prime turning FINISH feed along X; unit in pt_fin_radial_type."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-pt_fin_speed",t:"Word list",h:"pt_fin_speed",k:"pt fin speed pt_fin_speed",x:"Prime turning FINISH spindle speed - CSS when pt_fin_css is 1, else RPM."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-pt_rough_css",t:"Word list",h:"pt_rough_css",k:"pt rough css pt_rough_css",x:"1 = prime turning rough speed is CSS."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-pt_rough_feed_axial",t:"Word list",h:"pt_rough_feed_axial",k:"pt rough feed axial pt_rough_feed_axial",x:"Prime turning ROUGH feed along Z; unit in pt_rough_axial_type."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-pt_rough_feed_from",t:"Word list",h:"pt_rough_feed_from",k:"pt rough feed from pt_rough_feed_from",x:"user defined = the feeds as typed; chip thickness = Mastercam works them out from pt_rough_chip."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-pt_rough_feed_radial",t:"Word list",h:"pt_rough_feed_radial",k:"pt rough feed radial pt_rough_feed_radial",x:"Prime turning ROUGH feed along X; unit in pt_rough_radial_type."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-pt_rough_speed",t:"Word list",h:"pt_rough_speed",k:"pt rough speed pt_rough_speed",x:"Prime turning ROUGH spindle speed - CSS (SFM / m/min) when pt_rough_css is 1, else RPM. See also: primeturning ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-pt_strategy",t:"Word list",h:"pt_strategy",k:"pt strategy pt_strategy",x:"Prime turning roughing strategy."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-rough_step",t:"Word list",h:"rough_step",k:"rough step rough_step",x:"Face: rough stepover. Groove / plunge rough: step across the groove."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-rough_step_by",t:"Word list",h:"rough_step_by",k:"rough step by rough_step_by",x:"Groove rough step from: 0 number of steps, 1 step amount, 2 percent of tool width."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-cycle",t:"Word list",h:"cycle",k:"cycle cycle",x:"Drill cycle number."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-direction",t:"Word list",h:"direction",k:"direction direction",x:"Cut direction: 0 OD, 1 ID, 2 face, 3 back. Shown, never written."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-do_finish",t:"Word list",h:"do_finish",k:"do finish do_finish",x:"1 = take semi-finish passes."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-entry_amt",t:"Word list",h:"entry_amt",k:"entry amt entry_amt",x:"Rough: entry amount."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-exit_amt",t:"Word list",h:"exit_amt",k:"exit amt exit_amt",x:"Rough: exit amount."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-fin_n_cuts",t:"Word list",h:"fin_n_cuts",k:"fin n cuts fin_n_cuts",x:"Number of semi-finish passes."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-fin_step",t:"Word list",h:"fin_step",k:"fin step fin_step",x:"Semi-finish stepover."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-linear_tol",t:"Word list",h:"linear_tol",k:"linear tol linear_tol",x:"Tolerance for turning spline geometry into lines. Only matters on spline chains."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-manual_gcode",t:"Word list",h:"manual_gcode",k:"manual gcode manual_gcode",x:"Manual entry output: 1005 = as a comment, 1006 = as code."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-manual_save",t:"Word list",h:"manual_save",k:"manual save manual_save",x:"How the manual entry is saved. Shown, never written."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-manual_source",t:"Word list",h:"manual_source",k:"manual source manual_source",x:"Where the manual entry text comes from. Shown, never written."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-manual_text",t:"Word list",h:"manual_text",k:"manual text manual_text",x:"The manual entry's text (up to 3111 characters). Alt+Enter for a new line; with macros, double-click for a bigger editor."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-nonCuttingRegionAngle",t:"Word list",h:"nonCuttingRegionAngle",k:"nonCuttingRegionAngle nonCuttingRegionAngle",x:"Dynamic rough non-cutting region angle, degrees."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-overlap",t:"Word list",h:"overlap",k:"overlap overlap",x:"Rough: overlap amount between passes."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-radius",t:"Word list",h:"radius",k:"radius radius",x:"Dynamic rough toolpath radius. Change this and radius_percent follows."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-radius_percent",t:"Word list",h:"radius_percent",k:"radius percent radius_percent",x:"Toolpath radius as a percent of tool radius - calculated from radius. Typing here replaces the formula."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-rough_angle",t:"Word list",h:"rough_angle",k:"rough angle rough_angle",x:"Rough: cut angle, in RADIANS."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-zigzag",t:"Word list",h:"zigzag",k:"zigzag zigzag",x:"0 = one way, 1 = zigzag."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-clearance_pln",t:"Word list",h:"clearance_pln",k:"clearance pln clearance_pln",x:"Clearance plane value."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-feed_pln",t:"Word list",h:"feed_pln",k:"feed pln feed_pln",x:"Feed plane value."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-filter_on",t:"Word list",h:"filter_on",k:"filter on filter_on",x:"1 = arc filter / tolerance on: runs of short moves become arcs and longer lines."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-filter_reduce_type",t:"Word list",h:"filter_reduce_type",k:"filter reduce type filter_reduce_type",x:"0 = neither, 1 = reduce line tolerance, 2 = reduce arc tolerance."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-filter_tol",t:"Word list",h:"filter_tol",k:"filter tol filter_tol",x:"Arc filter tolerance."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-home_mode",t:"Word list",h:"home_mode",k:"home mode home_mode",x:"Home position: 0 from machine, 1 user defined, 2 from tool."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-retract_pln",t:"Word list",h:"retract_pln",k:"retract pln retract_pln",x:"Retract plane value."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-group_id",t:"Word list",h:"group_id",k:"group id group_id",x:"Toolpath group ID. Shown, never written."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-tplane_id",t:"Word list",h:"tplane_id",k:"tplane id tplane_id",x:"Tool plane ID. Shown, never written."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-wcs_id",t:"Word list",h:"wcs_id",k:"wcs id wcs_id",x:"Work coordinate system ID. Shown, never written."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-air_pct",t:"Word list",h:"air_pct",k:"air pct air_pct",x:"Share of the op's feed time cutting nothing (stock simulation). See also: air cutting ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-cut_dia",t:"Word list",h:"cut_dia",k:"cut dia cut_dia",x:"Mean cutting diameter over the feed moves (from the NCI) - where an RPM op's surface speed, and CSS's max_ss cap, are taken. A drill: the tool diameter. See also: sfm ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-cut_seconds_est",t:"Word list",h:"cut_seconds_est",k:"cut seconds est cut_seconds_est",x:"Feed time in seconds, LIVE - the cutting part of the estimate; the Tools page sums it per tool."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-edge_after",t:"Word list",h:"edge_after",k:"edge after edge_after",x:"Cut time (seconds) the tool's edge has done since its last flip, leaving this op - the next op of the same tool carries on from it."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-edge_limit",t:"Word list",h:"edge_limit",k:"edge limit edge_limit",x:"Cut time (seconds) an edge takes before a comment-less stop counts as a flip: the op's insp_time when filled in, else the tool's edge life on the Tools page (8:00). See also: edge life ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-flip_longest",t:"Word list",h:"flip_longest",k:"flip longest flip_longest",x:"The most feed time between flips (or from the start / to the end) - the longest any edge cuts."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-flips",t:"Word list",h:"flips",k:"flips flips",x:"Insert flips in the toolpath as it is: inspection stops whose comment changes the insert (CHANGE/ROTATE INSERT). See also: flip ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-flips_est",t:"Word list",h:"flips_est",k:"flips est flips_est",x:"Insert flips per part, LIVE: follows feeds, speeds and the inspection settings. Equal to flips until something changes. See also: flip ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-flips_part",t:"Word list",h:"flips_part",k:"flips part flips_part",x:"All the flips of the op per part, LIVE: commented (flips_est) + inferred (flips_uncommented). The Tools page sums it per tool. See also: flip ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-flips_uncommented",t:"Word list",h:"flips_uncommented",k:"flips uncommented flips_uncommented",x:"Flips from stops with NO comment, LIVE - inferred from the settings (they leave nothing in the NCI): one each time the edge has cut edge_limit, carrying short ops' time over to the tool's next op."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-flips_why",t:"Word list",h:"flips_why",k:"flips why flips_why",x:"Why each flip happened: time, distance, cuts (number of cuts, first cut, each depth) or end of the operation."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-insp_at_end",t:"Word list",h:"insp_at_end",k:"insp at end insp_at_end",x:"Tool inspection stop at the end of the operation."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-insp_between_cuts",t:"Word list",h:"insp_between_cuts",k:"insp between cuts insp_between_cuts",x:"1 = stop only between cuts (the real interval runs over the setting); 0 = stop mid-cut."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-insp_dist",t:"Word list",h:"insp_dist",k:"insp dist insp_dist",x:"Tool inspection: cut length between stops."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-insp_min_cut",t:"Word list",h:"insp_min_cut",k:"insp min cut insp_min_cut",x:"Mid-cut stops: a pass with less than this left is finished first."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-insp_mode",t:"Word list",h:"insp_mode",k:"insp mode insp_mode",x:"How stops are placed: between cuts only, or mid-cut (finishing a pass that is nearly done)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-insp_time",t:"Word list",h:"insp_time",k:"insp time insp_time",x:"Tool inspection: feed time between stops, as minutes:seconds (9:00) - or seconds (540). See also: insp time ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-mrr",t:"Word list",h:"mrr",k:"mrr mrr",x:"Metal removal rate while cutting, LIVE, in3/min (cm3/min metric). Turning: 12 x SFM x feed/rev x depth; drill: pi D^2/4 x feed/min; mill: ae x ap x feed/min (see mrr_basis). Follows speed, feed and depth edits. See also: mrr ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-mrr_avg",t:"Word list",h:"mrr_avg",k:"mrr avg mrr_avg",x:"Actual MRR over the whole operation, LIVE: removed / op time - rapids, leads and air cuts included. Follows feed and speed edits through the estimate. See also: mrr ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-mrr_basis",t:"Word list",h:"mrr_basis",k:"mrr basis mrr_basis",x:"What mrr is worked from: the depth-of-cut column, the diameter, and whether max_ss caps the speed. A mill op: its ae (contour: full tool width unless multi passes - an upper bound; dynamic: stepover) and ap."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-mrr_engaged",t:"Word list",h:"mrr_engaged",k:"mrr engaged mrr_engaged",x:"Actual MRR while in metal, LIVE: removed / (cut time less its air share). Set it beside mrr (theoretical) - a big gap says the depth or engagement is not what the parameters suggest. See also: mrr ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-removed",t:"Word list",h:"removed",k:"removed removed",x:"Material the op removes (in3, cm3 metric) - fixed by its toolpath, so feed and speed edits do not change it (a depth edit does only once the op is regenerated). From a 2D stock simulation where it covers the op, else mrr x cut time - see removed_from. See also: mrr ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-removed_check",t:"Word list",h:"removed_check",k:"removed check removed_check",x:"The stock simulation set against Mastercam's own stock boundaries: 'agrees', or both figures when they differ by more than 20% - usually a tool whose outline in Mastercam is not its real insert."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-removed_from",t:"Word list",h:"removed_from",k:"removed from removed_from",x:"Where removed comes from: the stock simulation (the toolpath swept through the stock), or an estimate (mrr x cut time, which counts air as cutting). Drill cycles, mill moves and ops after a stock flip are not simulated."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-xf_copies",t:"Word list",h:"xf_copies",k:"xf copies xf_copies",x:"A transform: how many copies of its source operations its toolpath holds (measured). A source: how many more times transforms cut it - the Tools page counts its tool time and flips that many more times."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-xf_detail",t:"Word list",h:"xf_detail",k:"xf detail xf_detail",x:"The transform in words: its kind and numbers (steps, angle or distance, about where)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-xf_instances",t:"Word list",h:"xf_instances",k:"xf instances xf_instances",x:"Copies the transform makes, as its dialog counts them: rotate - its steps; rectangular array - X steps x Y steps; mirror - 1."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-xf_make_ops",t:"Word list",h:"xf_make_ops",k:"xf make ops xf_make_ops",x:"1 = the transform makes new operations (and geometry); 0 = it transforms the toolpath only."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-xf_source_geom",t:"Word list",h:"xf_source_geom",k:"xf source geom xf_source_geom",x:"1 = transforms the source operations' geometry; 0 = their NCI."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-xf_sources",t:"Word list",h:"xf_sources",k:"xf sources xf_sources",x:"The op_idn of each operation the transform copies, as Mastercam lists them."},
{p:"words.html",a:"col-xf_type",t:"Word list",h:"xf_type",k:"xf type xf_type",x:"Transform kind: mirror, rotate or translate. Transforms are shown, never written."},
{p:"words.html",a:"t-insert",t:"Word list",h:"Insert",k:"Insert Insert",x:"The insert the tool carries, worked out from its shape. Type over it if it is wrong; tools with the same name count as one insert."},
{p:"words.html",a:"t-flips",t:"Word list",h:"Flips / part",k:"Flips / part Flips / part",x:"The tool's insert flips per part, from all its ops (live)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"t-cut-time",t:"Word list",h:"Cut time / part",k:"Cut time / part Cut time / part",x:"The tool's cutting time per part (live)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"t-longest",t:"Word list",h:"Longest between flips",k:"Longest between flips Longest between flips",x:"The most cutting time any edge of this tool does between flips."},
{p:"words.html",a:"t-inspection",t:"Word list",h:"Inspection",k:"Inspection Inspection",x:"What the tool's ops stop on, in words (“every 8:00 of cutting, at end”)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"t-edge-life",t:"Word list",h:"Edge life (fallback)",k:"Edge life (fallback) Edge life (fallback)",x:"Cut time one edge lasts, for ops that stop for inspection but set no insp_time . See edge life ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"t-edges",t:"Word list",h:"Edges per insert",k:"Edges per insert Edges per insert",x:"How many cutting edges one insert has (inserts table)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"t-inserts",t:"Word list",h:"Inserts / part",k:"Inserts / part Inserts / part",x:"Flips per part ÷ edges per insert, in whole inserts (a fraction for an insert that outlasts a part)."},
{p:"words.html",a:"t-cost",t:"Word list",h:"Cost per insert, Insert cost / part",k:"Cost per insert, Insert cost / part Cost per insert, Insert cost / part",x:"Type what one insert costs; the cost per part follows."},
{p:"words.html",a:"t-parts-per-edge",t:"Word list",h:"Parts per edge",k:"Parts per edge Parts per edge",x:"For an insert that lasts more than one part: how many parts one edge makes."},
{p:"words.html",a:"t-usual",t:"Word list",h:"Usual edge time",k:"Usual edge time Usual edge time",x:"How long one edge of this insert usually cuts before it is turned (inserts table). See usual edge time ."},
{p:"words.html",a:"t-ic",t:"Word list",h:"Insert size (IC)",k:"Insert size (IC) Insert size (IC)",x:"The insert's size across its inscribed circle – a round insert's diameter – in the part's units. Chip thinning uses it."},
{p:"words.html",a:"t-angle",t:"Word list",h:"Entering angle",k:"Entering angle Entering angle",x:"Degrees from the cutting edge to the feed, from the holder's ISO code (PCLNR: 95). 90 = square shoulder, no chip thinning. US lead angle = 90 minus this. See entering angle ."},
{p:"troubleshooting.html",a:"what",t:"Troubleshooting",h:"What this is for",k:"troubleshooting problem error not working help fix message no ribbon tab enable content macros disabled protected view unblock refused locked open in Excel stale times log crash Fix a problem Messages, and what to do about them.",x:"Something did not work, or a message came up. Find it below. Each topic page also has its own “What the messages mean”."},
{p:"troubleshooting.html",a:"when",t:"Troubleshooting",h:"When to use it",k:"",x:"When the tool does not do what you expect – before you give up on a change."},
{p:"troubleshooting.html",a:"steps",t:"Troubleshooting",h:"Steps",k:"",x:"Read the message word for word. Type a few of its words into the search box at the top of this page. Look for it in the list below. Nothing went into the part yet? Then nothing is lost: Excel edits stay in the workbook until you load. Something went into the part and it is wrong? Run Lathe params - undo last load."},
{p:"troubleshooting.html",a:"example",t:"Troubleshooting",h:"Worked example",k:"",x:"Problem: the workbook opened, but there is no Parameter Table tab on Excel's ribbon. Check What you see Fix The file name ends in .xlsx It was dumped without macros. Dump again with Include macros ticked (you get an .xlsm). A yellow bar under the ribbon Macros have been disabled Click Enable Content. A red bar Microsoft has blocked macros ... The file came from email or the internet. Close it, right-click the file, Properties, tick Unblock, OK, open it again. A yellow bar Protected View Click Enable Editing."},
{p:"troubleshooting.html",a:"messages",t:"Troubleshooting",h:"Problems and messages",k:"",x:"In Mastercam The commands are not in Mastercam at all See Install: both ParamTable.ft and the ParamTable folder must be in the Add-Ins folder, and Mastercam must be restarted. Save the part first ... Save the part (File, Save). The dump and load work beside the saved part file. The dump failed unexpectedly. Nothing was changed in the part. Something in the part upset the tool. Try dumping fewer ops to find which one, and tell whoever looks after the tool. The load failed unexpectedly. Check ParamTable.log ... The load stopped part way. ParamTable.log beside the part lists which ops were written. Run undo last load to put them back. ... could not be opened (is it still open in Excel?) Save in Excel first. If it still fails, close the workbook in Excel and load again. The load found no changes The workbook was not saved after the edits, or you picked an older file. Save, and pick the right file (the load starts on the one you dumped last). Refused rows in the load preview See Load: what the messages mean. The NC code still has the old feed The changed ops need regenerating (they are marked dirty). Regenerate, then post again. In Excel No Parameter Table tab See the worked example above. A cell will not take what I type The value is outside what the setting takes, or the cell is grey (read-only, or not for this kind of op). The message says which. See The main sheet. The cycle time did not change when I changed a depth Depths change the toolpath itself; the estimate can only follow feeds and speeds. See How the estimate works. Times or flips are blank, or needs_regen says yes The op needed regenerating when it was dumped. Regenerate in Mastercam and dump again. Flips per part look wrong See How insert flips are counted. Check the inspection comment and the edge life on the Tools page. Insert cost is blank Type the edges and the cost per insert in the inserts table. Select cells on the 'Lathe params' sheet first. Click the Lathe params tab and select cells in the op rows. Something went wrong: ... A ribbon command hit an error. Press Undo on the ribbon to take back anything it did, save your work, and tell whoever looks after the tool what the message said. The user manual is not installed ... The help folder is not beside the add-in. Copy the whole ParamTable folder from the zip again. I lost my edits Only if the workbook was closed without saving. Edits you saved are in the file; a saved scenario is too."},
{p:"troubleshooting.html",a:"related",t:"Troubleshooting",h:"Related",k:"",x:"Install the add-in Load the changes Undo and Undo a load Word list"}
];
